10.1371/journal.pone.0294325,dd0a356e6efa9ef9,6,,Conformity and Character,"Crutchfield, Richard S.",1955,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 6, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""record_type"": ""replication"", ""authors"": ""Crutchfield, Richard S."", ""year"": ""1955"", ""title"": ""Conformity and Character"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""outcome"": ""mixed""}, ""quote"": ""We randomized the groups’ response to five different political statements and find an average conformity rate of 38%. Hence, these results suggest that the original finding of Asch can also be generalized to matters of opinion. This result is in line with former evidence by Crutchfield [ 8 ], and Mallinson and Hatemi [ 9 ]. However, both of these studies had only small sample sizes of 50 and 58 subjects respectively, which called for further replication studies. Finally, we measured the Big Five, intelligence, self-esteem and social approval. With the exception of openness, our study finds no support that these personality traits are statistically significantly related to the susceptibility of group pressure."", ""location"": ""Section 5. Conclusion and discussion (line 170)"", ""reason"": ""Although framed as an extension relative to Asch's line task, the paper directly re-tests Crutchfield's (1955) published findings on political-opinion conformity (H3, derived in Section 2.2 from Crutchfield's 36-40 percentage-point effects with n=50) and individual-difference correlates of conformity (intellectual competence, self-esteem/ego strength, and need for social approval in Section 2.3, H4-H6) in a larger sample (N=202). The Conclusion explicitly states that Crutchfield's small sample size called for further replication studies and confirms the political-opinion conformity effect (38%), while finding no overall support for the intelligence, self-esteem, and social-approval associations, yielding a mixed outcome.""}","{""authors"": ""Crutchfield, Richard S."", ""year"": ""1955"", ""title"": ""Conformity and Character"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""However, both of these studies had only small sample sizes of 50 and 58 subjects respectively, which called for further replication studies."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Moreover, our study suggests that group pressure is not only influential in the simple line task but also when it comes to political opinions. We randomized the groups’ response to five different political statements and find an average conformity rate of 38%. Hence, these results suggest that the original finding of Asch can also be generalized to matters of opinion. This result is in line with former evidence by Crutchfield [ 8 ], and Mallinson and Hatemi [ 9 ]."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Political conformity agrees, but this paper also re-tests the intellectual competence, self-esteem and approval associations attributed to Crutchfield and reports no support for those traits overall.""}"
10.1371/journal.pone.0294325,dd0a356e6efa9ef9,7,10.1371/journal.pone.0196600,The effects of information and social conformity on opinion change,"Mallinson, Daniel J.; Hatemi, Peter K.",2018,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 7, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""record_type"": ""replication"", ""authors"": ""Mallinson, Daniel J.; Hatemi, Peter K."", ""year"": ""2018"", ""title"": ""The effects of information and social conformity on opinion change"", ""doi"": ""10.1371/journal.pone.0196600"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""outcome"": ""successful""}, ""quote"": ""We randomized the groups’ response to five different political statements and find an average conformity rate of 38%. Hence, these results suggest that the original finding of Asch can also be generalized to matters of opinion. This result is in line with former evidence by Crutchfield [ 8 ], and Mallinson and Hatemi [ 9 ]. However, both of these studies had only small sample sizes of 50 and 58 subjects respectively, which called for further replication studies."", ""location"": ""Section 5. Conclusion and discussion (line 170)"", ""reason"": ""The paper does not merely cite Mallinson and Hatemi (2018) as background motivation: Section 2.2 reviews their specific experimental finding (38% vs. 8% political opinion change under social pressure, n=58) to derive H3, Part 2 of the experiment tests political-opinion conformity under randomized unanimous confederate responses in a larger sample (N=202), and the Conclusion explicitly compares the resulting 38% conformity rate as confirming Mallinson and Hatemi (2018) while noting that their small sample size (n=58) called for further replication studies.""}","{""authors"": ""Mallinson, Daniel J.; Hatemi, Peter K."", ""year"": ""2018"", ""title"": ""The effects of information and social conformity on opinion change"", ""doi"": ""10.1371/journal.pone.0196600"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""However, both of these studies had only small sample sizes of 50 and 58 subjects respectively, which called for further replication studies."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Moreover, our study suggests that group pressure is not only influential in the simple line task but also when it comes to political opinions. We randomized the groups’ response to five different political statements and find an average conformity rate of 38%. Hence, these results suggest that the original finding of Asch can also be generalized to matters of opinion. This result is in line with former evidence by Crutchfield [ 8 ], and Mallinson and Hatemi [ 9 ]."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": """"}"
10.31820/pt.28.3.7,5435dc83d0805ac2,1,10.1111/j.2044-8309.1990.tb00892.x,"Knowing what to think by knowing who you are: Self-categorisation and the nature of norm formation, conformity and group polarisation","Abrams, D., Wetherell, M., Cochrane, S., Hogg, M. A., & Turner, J. C.",1990,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": ""10.1111/j.2044-8309.1990.tb00892.x"", ""title"": ""Knowing what to think by knowing who you are: Self-categorisation and the nature of norm formation, conformity and group polarisation"", ""authors"": ""Abrams, D., Wetherell, M., Cochrane, S., Hogg, M. A., & Turner, J. C."", ""year"": ""1990"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""successful""}, ""quote"": ""Consistent with the assumption of the SCT (Turner, 1991;Turner et al., 1987), participants exposed to the in-group majority showed the increase in conformity in comparison to the standard condition in which group identity was not salient. On the opposite, when the majority was presented as the out-group, the conformity effect significantly dropped. Thus, we replicated and extended past research (Abrahams et al., 1990), showing that self-categorization could play a determining role in conformity even in more minimal conditions, in which salient in-and out-group characteristics (i.e., ethnicity) were completely irrelevant for the task at hand."", ""location"": ""Discussion, line 22"", ""reason"": ""In addition to replicating the classic Asch conformity effect, the study explicitly adapts the design of Abrams et al. (1990) to re-test their finding that self-categorization (in-group vs. out-group majority membership) moderates conformity in the Asch paradigm without confounding similarity and competence (lines 6-7), and explicitly concludes that it replicated and extended Abrams et al. (1990) (line 22). This is an omitted target in the shipped dataset.""}","{""authors"": ""Abrams, D., Wetherell, M., Cochrane, S., Hogg, M. A., & Turner, J. C."", ""year"": ""1990"", ""title"": ""Knowing what to think by knowing who you are: Self-categorisation and the nature of norm formation, conformity and group polarisation"", ""doi"": ""10.1111/j.2044-8309.1990.tb00892.x"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Thus, we replicated and extended past research (Abrahams et al., 1990), showing that self-categorization could play a determining role in conformity even in more minimal conditions, in which salient in-and out-group characteristics (i.e., ethnicity) were completely irrelevant for the task at hand."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Thus, we replicated and extended past research (Abrahams et al., 1990), showing that self-categorization could play a determining role in conformity even in more minimal conditions, in which salient in-and out-group characteristics (i.e., ethnicity) were completely irrelevant for the task at hand."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.3102/0013189x20940304,15b01ee69a8e149c,0,,Equality of educational opportunity study.,"Coleman, J. S., Campbell, E., Hobson, C., McPartland, F., Mood, A., & Weinfeld, F.",1966,full_text,,error,obvious,"{""target_index"": 0, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""obvious"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Coleman, J. S., Campbell, E., Hobson, C., McPartland, F., Mood, A., & Weinfeld, F."", ""year"": ""1966"", ""title"": ""Equality of educational opportunity study."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined""}, ""quote"": ""Our results replicate both the original Coleman Report ICCs, as well as those from Bryk and Raudenbush’s study."", ""location"": ""Results"", ""reason"": ""The results explicitly replicate Coleman Report ICCs and name the distinct 1966 report in the references. The sole shipped original is Bryk and Raudenbush (1988). This is a direct empirical retest, so Luna's background label is contradicted by the source. Parent reviewed the cited results/purpose and reference identity against all supplied shipped originals. This decision confirms the missing paper-level pair only; its overall outcome has not been adjudicated.""}","{""authors"": ""Coleman, J. S., Campbell, E., Hobson, C., McPartland, F., Mood, A., & Weinfeld, F."", ""year"": ""1966"", ""title"": ""Equality of educational opportunity study."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Our results replicate both the original Coleman Report ICCs, as well as those from Bryk and Raudenbush’s study."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Our results replicate both the original Coleman Report ICCs, as well as those from Bryk and Raudenbush’s study. In the left column of Table 1 (variation in achievement levels), our results are largely consistent with the Coleman Report: Only 23.7% of the variance in math achievement levels lies between schools (21.4% in ELA)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicit overall replication verdict covers both target studies, supported by target-specific comparisons. Scaling sensitivity and cautious interpretation do not negate the stated successful replication.""}"
10.1017/s000305540505166x,7ca2f3f9da21d2d9,1,,Do Get-Out-the-Vote Calls Reduce Turnout? The Importance of Statistical Methods for Field Experiments.,"Imai, Kosuke.",2005,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": """", ""title"": ""Do Get-Out-the-Vote Calls Reduce Turnout? The Importance of Statistical Methods for Field Experiments."", ""authors"": ""Imai, Kosuke."", ""year"": ""2005""}, ""quote"": ""Replication of Professor Imai’s (2005) analysis reveals a series of errors that call into question the magnitude, significance, and robustness of his matching estimates."", ""location"": ""Introduction, p. 301 (see also Table 2 and pp. 308-310)"", ""reason"": ""Omitted target: Under the codebook rules for replication chains and earlier replication/reanalysis attempts, an earlier reanalysis is a target when the current paper directly re-tests its findings. Gerber and Green (2005) directly re-runs Imai (2005)'s propensity score matching code and analysis on the dataset Imai analyzed (Table 2, pp. 308-310), demonstrating computational discrepancies and robustness failures, and also tests Imai's 6.5 percentage point estimate in the 2002 replication experiment.""}","{""authors"": ""Imai, Kosuke."", ""year"": ""2005"", ""title"": ""Do Get-Out-the-Vote Calls Reduce Turnout? The Importance of Statistical Methods for Field Experiments."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Replication of Professor Imai’s (2005) analysis reveals a series of errors that call into question the magnitude, significance, and robustness of his matching estimates."", ""outcome"": """", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": ""computational issues"", ""outcome_computational_quote"": ""Table 9 of Imai 2005 exaggerates the significance of the phone effect by reporting a coefficient of 6.5 with a standard error of 3.2. These numbers imply a t-ratio of 2.03, which tells the reader at a glance that the effect is statistically significant using a two-sided test (p < .05). When these numbers are replicated using 500,000 bootstrap samples rather than 500, the correct estimate is 6.2, with a t-ratio of 1.87 (see Table 2)."", ""outcome_robustness"": ""robustness challenges"", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": ""The estimated effects of phone calls fall to insignificance when based on the actual sample. His matching program generates an estimate of 5.1 (SE = 3.3) when performing one-to-five matching on the actual sample, and this number drops to 3.6 (SE = 4.5) when one-to-five matching is performed with replacement."", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Re-running the original matching analysis yields substantive numerical discrepancies and inflated reported significance. Alternative matching specifications and analysis of actual rather than bootstrap samples remove significance. This target is itself an earlier reanalysis, directly re-tested here.""}"
10.1093/carcin/bgw047,95c3723ecf6c6ec1,0,,Common variants on 9q22.33 and 14q13.3 predispose to thyroid cancer in European populations.,"Gudmundsson J., et al.",2009,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 0, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Gudmundsson J., et al."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""Common variants on 9q22.33 and 14q13.3 predispose to thyroid cancer in European populations."", ""doi"": """", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""mixed""}, ""quote"": ""We confirmed the well-known association with DTC of the 9q22.31 region that includes the FOXE1 gene ( 9–12 ). In this independent and separate case–control study conducted in Germany, we replicated associations with PTC risk that we reported previously in SERPINA5 ( 21 ) and FTO ( 17 ) genes. Other candidate variants in EVPL , TICAM1 and SCARB1 previously identified ( 17 , 21 ) were also associated with PTC risk. We found several new associations between SNPs in SERPINA5 , FTO , TICAM1 and HSPA6 genes, and risk of FTC. In contrast to several studies, we did not observe associations for NKX2-1 SNPs ( 9 , 25 , 26 )."", ""location"": ""Discussion"", ""reason"": ""Omitted target: SNPs in FOXE1 and NKX2-1 were selected for validation from reference 9, with FOXE1 confirmed and NKX2-1 not observed (mixed).""}","{""authors"": ""Gudmundsson J., et al."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""Common variants on 9q22.33 and 14q13.3 predispose to thyroid cancer in European populations."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We confirmed the well-known association with DTC of the 9q22.31 region that includes the FOXE1 gene ( 9–12 )."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We confirmed the well-known association with DTC of the 9q22.31 region that includes the FOXE1 gene ( 9–12 ).  In this independent and separate case–control study conducted in Germany, we replicated associations with PTC risk that we reported previously in SERPINA5 ( 21 ) and FTO ( 17 ) genes. Other candidate variants in EVPL , TICAM1 and SCARB1 previously identified ( 17 , 21 ) were also associated with PTC risk. We found several new associations between SNPs in SERPINA5 , FTO , TICAM1 and HSPA6 genes, and risk of FTC. In contrast to several studies, we did not observe associations for NKX2-1 SNPs ( 9 , 25 , 26 )."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Verdict scoped to this cited original; no aggregate count transferred. For reference 25, the explicit NKX2-1 contrast is failed; no explicit comparison settles its FOXE1 finding.""}"
