; no explicit comparison settles its FOXE1 finding.""}"
10.1093/carcin/bgw047,95c3723ecf6c6ec1,9,,Common single nucleotide polymorphisms in genes related to immune function and risk of papillary thyroid cancer.,"Brenner A.V., et al.",2013,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 9, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Brenner A.V., et al."", ""year"": ""2013"", ""title"": ""Common single nucleotide polymorphisms in genes related to immune function and risk of papillary thyroid cancer."", ""doi"": """", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""successful""}, ""quote"": ""In this independent and separate case–control study conducted in Germany, we replicated associations with PTC risk that we reported previously in SERPINA5 ( 21 ) and FTO ( 17 ) genes. Other candidate variants in EVPL , TICAM1 and SCARB1 previously identified ( 17 , 21 ) were also associated with PTC risk."", ""location"": ""Discussion"", ""reason"": ""Omitted target: reference 21 is one of the NCI i-Select studies (16–21) directly replicated, and its SERPINA5 and other candidate variant associations with PTC were confirmed.""}","{""authors"": ""Brenner A.V., et al."", ""year"": ""2013"", ""title"": ""Common single nucleotide polymorphisms in genes related to immune function and risk of papillary thyroid cancer."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The main objective of the current study was to replicate the most significant findings for PTC that we reported previously from an analysis of 1129 candidate genes based on 17525 tag single-nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) from the National Cancer Institute’s (NCI) rare cancer i-Select study ( 16–21 )."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In this independent and separate case–control study conducted in Germany, we replicated associations with PTC risk that we reported previously in SERPINA5 ( 21 ) and FTO ( 17 ) genes. Other candidate variants in EVPL , TICAM1 and SCARB1 previously identified ( 17 , 21 ) were also associated with PTC risk."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Verdict scoped to this cited original; no aggregate count transferred. For reference 25, the explicit NKX2-1 contrast is failed; no explicit comparison settles its FOXE1 finding.""}"
10.1037/edu0000146,701e7e90a9964039,1,10.1037/a0019506,Enhancing interest and performance with a utility value intervention.,"Hulleman, C. S., Godes, O., Hendricks, B. L., & Harackiewicz, J. M.",2010,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": ""10.1037/a0019506"", ""title"": ""Enhancing interest and performance with a utility value intervention."", ""authors"": ""Hulleman, C. S., Godes, O., Hendricks, B. L., & Harackiewicz, J. M."", ""year"": ""2010"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""mixed""}, ""quote"": ""The utility value interventions utilized in the current studies were based on the self-generated utility value interventions used by Hulleman and colleagues (e.g., Hulleman et al., 2010; Hulleman & Harackiewicz, 2009). In addition to replicating this prior research, we extend it in five ways."", ""location"": ""introduction (Current Studies, lines 517-524)"", ""reason"": ""Omitted target: Hulleman et al. (2010) (DOI: 10.1037/a0019506) is an independent original paper explicitly cited throughout the introduction, methods, and discussion alongside Hulleman & Harackiewicz (2009) as prior utility value intervention research replicated in Study 2, and shares the same 'mixed' partial-replication verdict in the Study 2 Discussion.""}","{""authors"": ""Hulleman, C. S., Godes, O., Hendricks, B. L., & Harackiewicz, J. M."", ""year"": ""2010"", ""title"": ""Enhancing interest and performance with a utility value intervention."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/a0019506"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""2"", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Although the research generated by the expectancy-value framework has been largely correlational (Wigfield & Cambria, 2010), recent classroom studies reveal that interventions designed to enhance perceptions of value can increase both interest and course perfor- mance (e.g., Hulleman & Harackiewicz, 2009; Hulleman, Godes, Hendricks, & Harackiewicz, 2010). The research presented herein replicates and extends this prior work by further investigating a theory-based intervention designed to enhance student motivation and performance."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The results of Study 2 partially replicated prior research dem- onstrating that a theoretically guided intervention based on the expectancy-value framework could enhance student learning out- comes (Hulleman & Harackiewicz, 2009; Hulleman et al., 2010)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly give a partial-replication verdict over both cited prior intervention papers; interest/performance benefits coexist with an unexpected mechanism.""}"
10.1075/target.18159.ola,541c384d0ea1859d,0,,Replications in Psychology Research,"Makel, Matthew C., Jonathan A. Plucker, and Boyd Hegarty.",2012,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 0, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Makel, Matthew C., Jonathan A. Plucker, and Boyd Hegarty."", ""year"": ""2012"", ""title"": ""Replications in Psychology Research"", ""doi"": """", ""type"": ""replication"", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""2"", ""outcome"": ""mixed""}, ""quote"": ""To complement the survey results, a bibliometric approach was used to identify replications in the TIS literature by carrying out a conceptual replication of Makel, Plucker, and Hegarty’s (2012) study on replication in psychology research. While the original study was conducted by analyzing only 100 journals with the highest 5-year impact factor in the field of psychology, in the present study, the bibliometric analysis was carried out using BITRA (Franco Aixelá 2001–2019)."", ""location"": ""Section 3.2 Study 2: Bibliometric analysis of replications in BITRA, lines 836-843 (with comparisons in Section 5.2, lines 2368-2425)"", ""reason"": ""Omitted target in the shipped dataset. Study 2 explicitly conducts a conceptual replication of Makel, Plucker, and Hegarty (2012) using the BITRA database and directly compares its bibliometric findings against Makel et al. (2012) in Section 5.2. Those comparisons are mixed: the author notes that the overall published replication rate is higher in psychology (1.07% vs. 0.06% in BITRA) and that self-replications make up 52.9% of replications in Makel et al. (2012) versus 30.4% in BITRA (where replications of others' studies predominate at 65.2%), while the low proportion of exact replications is described as not very different from Makel et al. (8.7% in BITRA vs. 14% in psychology).""}","{""authors"": ""Makel, Matthew C., Jonathan A. Plucker, and Boyd Hegarty."", ""year"": ""2012"", ""title"": ""Replications in Psychology Research"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""To complement the survey results, a bibliometric approach was used to identify replications in the TIS\n\nliterature by carrying out a conceptual replication of Makel, Plucker, and Hegarty’s (2012) study on\nreplication in psychology research."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In psychology,\n\na discipline in which replication is more common than in empirical TIS, the percentage of replications\n\npublished in journals was 1.07% (Makel, Plucker, and Hegarty 2012)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The quoted sentence marks one difference. Elsewhere the authors describe exact-replication proportions as not very different from Makel et al.; hence mixed.""}"
