results of previous\nstudies (e.g., Strand and Eldevik 2018) limitations can\nbe noted."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""This study demonstrated the successful replication of\nthe practical functional assessment and treatment de-\nscribed previously in several studies by a limited num-\nber of researchers within an early intensive behavior\nintervention (EIBI) program in a clinical setting."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly conclude successful replication and specifically name Strand and Eldevik as a study whose results replicated. Limitations do not retract that verdict.""}"
10.3758/s13428-023-02329-5,49c906aa1fa610be,1,10.1037/xge0000037,When goals conflict with values: Counterproductive attentional and oculomotor capture by reward-related stimuli.,"Le Pelley, M. E., Pearson, D., Griffiths, O., & Beesley, T.",2015,full_text,,error,obvious,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""obvious"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Le Pelley, M. E., Pearson, D., Griffiths, O., & Beesley, T."", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": ""When goals conflict with values: Counterproductive attentional and oculomotor capture by reward-related stimuli."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/xge0000037"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined""}, ""quote"": ""These two results replicate previous findings with Le Pelley’s paradigm (Le Pelley et al.,\n2015\n), where the VMAC effect emerged over trials, even when the reward-signals are always task irrelevant, and also replicate Watson et al. (\n2019a\n), who showed that once established, the VMAC effect remains present regardless of the current informational value of the reward-related stimuli."", ""location"": ""Discussion"", ""reason"": ""The discussion expressly attributes the reproduced trial-wise reward capture result to Le Pelley et al. (2015), separately from Watson et al. (2019). The references identify DOI 10.1037/xge0000037; the only shipped pair is Watson (10.1371/journal.pone.0226284). Parent reviewed the cited results/purpose and reference identity against all supplied shipped originals. This decision confirms the missing paper-level pair only; its overall outcome has not been adjudicated.""}","{""authors"": ""Le Pelley, M. E., Pearson, D., Griffiths, O., & Beesley, T."", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": ""When goals conflict with values: Counterproductive attentional and oculomotor capture by reward-related stimuli."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/xge0000037"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The first aim of the present study was to replicate and extend the results of Watson et al. (\n2019a\n), which demonstrated that the VMAC effect could be observed in conditions where a high reward predicting singleton had always been response irrelevant and held no informational value. In our study, we showed that the VMAC effect increased over time in the rewarded phase, that is, we observed a progressive increase in RTs when the high valued singleton acted as singleton distractor compared to the low valued singleton. Furthermore, when reward feedback was omitted in the unrewarded phase, the VMAC effect remained constant, without any evidence of reduction over trials. These two results replicate previous findings with Le Pelley’s paradigm (Le Pelley et al.,\n2015\n), where the VMAC effect emerged over trials, even when the reward-signals are always task irrelevant, and also replicate Watson et al. (\n2019a\n), who showed that once established, the VMAC effect remains present regardless of the current informational value of the reward-related stimuli."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The first aim of the present study was to replicate and extend the results of Watson et al. (\n2019a\n), which demonstrated that the VMAC effect could be observed in conditions where a high reward predicting singleton had always been response irrelevant and held no informational value. In our study, we showed that the VMAC effect increased over time in the rewarded phase, that is, we observed a progressive increase in RTs when the high valued singleton acted as singleton distractor compared to the low valued singleton. Furthermore, when reward feedback was omitted in the unrewarded phase, the VMAC effect remained constant, without any evidence of reduction over trials. These two results replicate previous findings with Le Pelley’s paradigm (Le Pelley et al.,\n2015\n), where the VMAC effect emerged over trials, even when the reward-signals are always task irrelevant, and also replicate Watson et al. (\n2019a\n), who showed that once established, the VMAC effect remains present regardless of the current informational value of the reward-related stimuli."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly state that their new-sample results replicate this finding.""}"
10.3390/jcm10010052,0e4704e600fb03ca,0,10.1192/bjo.2020.109,"Anxiety, depression, traumatic stress and COVID-19-related anxiety in the UK general population during the COVID-19 pandemic.","Shevlin, M.; McBride, O.; Murphy, J.; Miller, J.G.; Hartman, T.K.; Levita, L.; Mason, L.; Martinez, A.P.; McKay, R.; Stocks, T.V.A.; et al.",2020,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 0, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": ""10.1192/bjo.2020.109"", ""title"": ""Anxiety, depression, traumatic stress and COVID-19-related anxiety in the UK general population during the COVID-19 pandemic."", ""authors"": ""Shevlin, M.; McBride, O.; Murphy, J.; Miller, J.G.; Hartman, T.K.; Levita, L.; Mason, L.; Martinez, A.P.; McKay, R.; Stocks, T.V.A.; et al."", ""year"": ""2020"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""mixed""}, ""quote"": ""Summarizing, these results are in line with those of the UK study about anxiety, depression, and traumatic stress [19]. The UK and Italian data identify factors associated with worst psychological health: being a woman, younger age, having children, preexisting health issues of self or someone close, and the moderate to high perceived risk of contracting COVID-19 within one month. Unlike Italy, in the UK lower household incomes and having (had) the COVID-19 were associated with worst psychological health outcomes."", ""location"": ""Discussion, line 49 (see also Aims, line 14; Analytic Methods, line 34; Discussion, line 44; References, line 288)"", ""reason"": ""Omitted target. The paper explicitly re-tests two distinct UK C19PRC empirical papers [19,20]. In Section 3.2 (line 34, 41) and the Discussion (line 44: 'In a second part of the analysis, we replicated the approach by Shevlin et al. [19]'), the authors replicate the three binary logistic regression models from Shevlin et al. (2020) [19] (10.1192/bjo.2020.109) in the Italian sample and report mixed results (line 49: several risk factors matched the UK study, whereas lower household income and having had COVID-19 were associated with worse outcomes in the UK unlike Italy). Because [19] is an independent published paper whose findings are directly re-tested, it must be coded as a separate replication target.""}","{""authors"": ""Shevlin, M.; McBride, O.; Murphy, J.; Miller, J.G.; Hartman, T.K.; Levita, L.; Mason, L.; Martinez, A.P.; McKay, R.; Stocks, T.V.A.; et al."", ""year"": ""2020"", ""title"": ""Anxiety, depression, traumatic stress and COVID-19-related anxiety in the UK general population during the COVID-19 pandemic."", ""doi"": ""10.1192/bjo.2020.109"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In a second part of the analysis, we replicated the approach by Shevlin et al. [19]."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Summarizing, these results are in line with those of the UK study about anxiety, depression, and traumatic stress [19]. The UK and Italian data identify factors associated with worst psychological health: being a woman, younger age, having children, preexisting health issues of self or someone close, and the moderate to high perceived risk of contracting COVID-19 within one month. Unlike Italy, in the UK lower household incomes and having (had) the COVID-19 were associated with worst psychological health outcomes."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly identify matching risk factors and contrasting findings; the first sentence concerns these results rather than an overall verdict on the whole replication.""}"
10.1017/rdc.2024.52,2cb7ebb539e144f1,1,,A signature of cosmic-ray increase in AD 774–775 from tree rings in Japan.,"Miyake F, Nagaya K, Masuda K and Nakamura T",2012,full_text,,error,obvious,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""obvious"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Miyake F, Nagaya K, Masuda K and Nakamura T"", ""year"": ""2012"", ""title"": ""A signature of cosmic-ray increase in AD 774–775 from tree rings in Japan."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined""}, ""quote"": ""Firstly, we present new 14C measurements over the period from 771–779 CE, spanning the years of the 775 CE event. Our results show the same sharp rise shown by Miyake et al. (2012), even though the Δ14C values are on average higher than the ones reported from the original study."", ""location"": ""Results / discussion"", ""reason"": ""The new 771–779 CE measurements directly retest Miyake et al. (2012)'s 775 CE increase, separately from the 1052 CE Brehm comparison. The reference identifies the 2012 Nature paper. The sole shipped original is Brehm et al. (2021), DOI 10.1038/s41561-020-00674-0). Parent reviewed the cited results/purpose and reference identity against all supplied shipped originals. This decision confirms the missing paper-level pair only; its overall outcome has not been adjudicated.""}","{""authors"": ""Miyake F, Nagaya K, Masuda K and Nakamura T"", ""year"": ""2012"", ""title"": ""A signature of cosmic-ray increase in AD 774–775 from tree rings in Japan."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Firstly, we present new 14C measurements over the period from 771–779 CE, spanning the years of the 775 CE event. Our results show the same sharp rise shown by Miyake et al. (2012), even though the Δ14C values are on average higher than the ones reported from the original study."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Firstly, we present new 14C measurements over the period from 771–779 CE, spanning the years of the 775 CE event. Our results show the same sharp rise shown by Miyake et al. (2012), even though the Δ14C values are on average higher than the ones reported from the original study."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.1080/0003684042000229532,236d200a82c3a67f,1,,"Career mobility, job match, and overeducation",Robst J.,1995,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Robst J."", ""year"": ""1995"", ""title"": ""Career mobility, job match, and overeducation"", ""doi"": """", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""successful""}, ""quote"": ""In Section 4.3, we extend Robst’s (1995) test for robustness by using an objective, as well as a subjective, measure of training participation. Both measures confirm Robst’s findings that overeducated (undereducated) workers indeed receive less (more) training than correctly allocated ones."", ""location"": ""Section 1 (Introduction), p. 3, lines 190-198; Section 4.3, pp. 16-17, lines 864-920"", ""reason"": ""Omitted target in shipped data. Beyond citing Robst (1995) as background, the paper explicitly re-tests Robst's (1995) empirical finding on training participation in Section 4.3 (Tables 5 and 6) using the same subjective survey question in the German GSOEP sample as well as an objective formal training indicator, concluding that both measures confirm Robst's findings.""}","{""authors"": ""Robst J."", ""year"": ""1995"", ""title"": ""Career mobility, job match, and overeducation"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In Section 4.3, we extend Robst’s (1995) test for robustness by using an objective, as well as a subjective, measure of training participation."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Both measures confirm Robst’s findings that overeducated (undereducated) workers indeed receive less (more) training than correctly allocated ones."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""introduction"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.5964/jnc.7029,7113daab150388e1,0,10.1016/0010-0277(90)90003-3,Children's understanding of counting,"Wynn, K.",1990,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 0, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""classification"": ""omitted target"", ""doi"": ""10.1016/0010-0277(90)90003-3"", ""title"": ""Children's understanding of counting"", ""authors"": ""Wynn, K."", ""year"": ""1990"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""mixed""}, ""quote"": ""The present work focused on the GaN from Wynn's (1990Wynn's ( , 1992) ) studies with no intention to test the replicability of results coming from the other tasks (i.e., the Point-to-x task or the colour control task were not administered)."", ""location"": ""Study 1, Materials (line 17)"", ""reason"": ""Omitted target. The authors explicitly frame their work as a conceptual replication of the Give-a-Number (GaN) observations from both Wynn (1990) and Wynn (1992), and in Study 1 they directly re-test Wynn's (1990) counter vs. grabber strategy predictions (line 30) as well as the all-at-once generalization assumption attributed to Wynn (1990, 1992) (line 28), finding mixed support (confirming that large-number knowers count more frequently for large than small numbers, but finding sequential acquisition of large number words and no difference between terminal-counters and terminal-grabbers in reaching large-number knowledge).""}","{""authors"": ""Wynn, K."", ""year"": ""1990"", ""title"": ""Children's understanding of counting"", ""doi"": ""10.1016/0010-0277(90)90003-3"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1"", ""evidence_quote"": ""The present work focused on the GaN from Wynn's (1990Wynn's ( , 1992) ) studies with no intention to test the replicability of results coming from the other tasks (i.e., the Point-to-x task or the colour control task were not administered)."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Supporting this view, Large-numbers knowers (4-to 7-knowers) were found to be more likely to resort to counting for large number words than for small ones in study 1. In addition, several children switched from a grabbing to a counting strategy around four. However, the majority of participants kept a consistent strategy all along the task and many of them still succeeded at giving large numbers with a grabbing strategy. Thus, the strategy, at least as coded here, could not be considered as what makes the difference to acquire the cardinal knowledge of large number words."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly report agreement with some original observations and disagreement with others; no overall replication verdict supersedes those comparisons.""}"
10.1111/cdev.12774,c931e17eb50f6ca0,1,,Dysregulated Fear Predicts Social Wariness and Social Anxiety Symptoms during Kindergarten,"Buss KA, Davis EL, Kiel EJ, Brooker RJ, Beekman C, Early MC",2013,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": """", ""title"": ""Dysregulated Fear Predicts Social Wariness and Social Anxiety Symptoms during Kindergarten"", ""authors"": ""Buss KA, Davis EL, Kiel EJ, Brooker RJ, Beekman C, Early MC"", ""year"": ""2013"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""successful""}, ""quote"": ""The goals of the present study included (1) replication of identifying a pattern of dysregulated fear in toddlers (Buss, 2011) in a new sample; (2) replication of the prediction from toddler dysregulated fear to elevated social inhibition (Buss, 2011; Buss et al., 2013); and (3) extending our understanding of the implications of dysregulated fear for children’s development by linking this pattern of high fearfulness in low-threat contexts to physiological reactivity as a putative psychophysiological marker of dysregulated emotion processes in toddlerhood. Results were largely consistent with expectations—we were able to replicate the identification of children with dysregulated fear in this new sample, showed that this pattern of fear responding predicted inhibition across early childhood, and identified modest evidence for the idea that children who manifest a dysregulated fear pattern of behavior would also show a distinct pattern of physiological responding to laboratory challenges."", ""location"": ""discussion"", ""reason"": ""The paper explicitly names Buss et al. (2013) alongside Buss (2011) as an original target whose finding that toddler dysregulated fear predicts elevated social inhibition was successfully replicated.""}","{""authors"": ""Buss KA, Davis EL, Kiel EJ, Brooker RJ, Beekman C, Early MC"", ""year"": ""2013"", ""title"": ""Dysregulated Fear Predicts Social Wariness and Social Anxiety Symptoms during Kindergarten"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The goals of the present study included (1) replication of identifying a pattern of dysregulated fear in toddlers (Buss, 2011) in a new sample; (2) replication of the prediction from toddler dysregulated fear to elevated social inhibition (Buss, 2011; Buss et al., 2013); and (3) extending our understanding of the implications of dysregulated fear for children’s development by linking this pattern of high fearfulness in low-threat contexts to physiological reactivity as a putative psychophysiological marker of dysregulated emotion processes in toddlerhood. Results were largely consistent with expectations—we were able to replicate the identification of children with dysregulated fear in this new sample, showed that this pattern of fear responding predicted inhibition across early childhood, and identified modest evidence for the idea that children who manifest a dysregulated fear pattern of behavior would also show a distinct pattern of physiological responding to laboratory challenges."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The present study, which replicates and extends our previous work ([blinded for review]), contributes to this literature by demonstrating robustness in this pattern of fear as a marker of risk for anxiety-related problems. We demonstrated, in a second cohort of children, that a dysregulated pattern of fearful behavior - characterized by high fear in low threat situations – is associated with social inhibition across a 2-year period and is also associated with a different pattern of cardiac reactivity."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The stated replication goals explicitly name this original; the overall verdict supports dysregulated fear and its longitudinal association with inhibition. Differences in curve modeling and new RSA analyses do not reverse this verdict.""}"
10.7554/elife.73430,58f7736b9d35d428,0,10.1016/j.cell.2010.02.027,A chromatin-mediated reversible drug-tolerant state in cancer cell subpopulations,Sharma SV Lee DY Li B Quinlan MP Takahashi F Maheswaran S McDermott U Azizian N Zou L Fischbach MA Wong KK Brandstetter K Wittner B Ramaswamy S Classon M Settleman J,2010,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 0, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": ""10.1016/j.cell.2010.02.027"", ""title"": ""A chromatin-mediated reversible drug-tolerant state in cancer cell subpopulations"", ""authors"": ""Sharma SV Lee DY Li B Quinlan MP Takahashi F Maheswaran S McDermott U Azizian N Zou L Fischbach MA Wong KK Brandstetter K Wittner B Ramaswamy S Classon M Settleman J"", ""year"": ""2010"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""uninformative""}, ""quote"": ""As part of the Reproducibility Project: Cancer Biology, we published a Registered Report ( Haven et al., 2016 ) that described how we intended to replicate selected experiments from the paper ‘A chromatin-mediated reversible drug-tolerant state in cancer cell subpopulations’ ( Sharma et al., 2010 ). We attempted to independently replicate experiments that tested the phenotype of a small subpopulation of ‘drug-tolerant persister’ cells (DTPs) that are generated when PC9 cells, a protein-tyrosine kinase inhibitor (TKI)-sensitive non-small cell lung cancer cell line, is exposed to high concentrations of TKIs."", ""location"": ""Results — Partial replication: a chromatin-mediated reversible drug-tolerant state in cancer cell subpopulations"", ""reason"": ""Omitted target: this paper reports new experimental replication data (Figure 1, Materials and methods, OSF dataset) re-testing Sharma et al. (2010), with an explicit uninformative outcome summary stating that the preliminary data do not address the central claim because the remaining experiments were not continued.""}","{""authors"": ""Sharma SV Lee DY Li B Quinlan MP Takahashi F Maheswaran S McDermott U Azizian N Zou L Fischbach MA Wong KK Brandstetter K Wittner B Ramaswamy S Classon M Settleman J"", ""year"": ""2010"", ""title"": ""A chromatin-mediated reversible drug-tolerant state in cancer cell subpopulations"", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.cell.2010.02.027"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""As part of the Reproducibility Project: Cancer Biology, we published a Registered Report ( Haven et al., 2016 ) that described how we intended to replicate selected experiments from the paper ‘A chromatin-mediated reversible drug-tolerant state in cancer cell subpopulations’ ( Sharma et al., 2010 )."", ""outcome"": ""uninformative"", ""outcome_quote"": ""To summarize, this replication attempt obtained preliminary outcomes although the experiments were not continued and thus these data do not address whether DTPs maintained viability through IGF-1 receptor signaling and an altered chromatin state dependent on the histone demethylase KDM5A."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly report inability to adjudicate the central finding."", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """"}"
10.7554/elife.73430,58f7736b9d35d428,5,10.1038/nature10599,Senescence surveillance of pre-malignant hepatocytes limits liver cancer development,Kang TW Yevsa T Woller N Hoenicke L Wuestefeld T Dauch D Hohmeyer A Gereke M Rudalska R Potapova A Iken M Vucur M Weiss S Heikenwalder M Khan S Gil J Bruder D Manns M Schirmacher P Tacke F Ott M Luedde T Longerich T Kubicka S Zender L,2011,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 5, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": ""10.1038/nature10599"", ""title"": ""Senescence surveillance of pre-malignant hepatocytes limits liver cancer development"", ""authors"": ""Kang TW Yevsa T Woller N Hoenicke L Wuestefeld T Dauch D Hohmeyer A Gereke M Rudalska R Potapova A Iken M Vucur M Weiss S Heikenwalder M Khan S Gil J Bruder D Manns M Schirmacher P Tacke F Ott M Luedde T Longerich T Kubicka S Zender L"", ""year"": ""2011"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""uninformative""}, ""quote"": ""As part of the Reproducibility Project: Cancer Biology, we published a Registered Report ( Raouf et al., 2015 ) that described how we intended to replicate selected experiments from the paper ‘Senescence surveillance of pre-malignant hepatocytes limits liver cancer development’ ( Kang et al., 2011 )."", ""location"": ""Results — Partial replication: senescence surveillance of premalignant hepatocytes limits liver cancer development"", ""reason"": ""Omitted target: this paper reports new experimental replication data in mice (Figure 6, Materials and methods, OSF dataset) re-testing Kang et al. (2011), with an explicit uninformative outcome summary stating that low Nras expression confounded interpretation and the data are unable to address the original claim.""}","{""authors"": ""Kang TW Yevsa T Woller N Hoenicke L Wuestefeld T Dauch D Hohmeyer A Gereke M Rudalska R Potapova A Iken M Vucur M Weiss S Heikenwalder M Khan S Gil J Bruder D Manns M Schirmacher P Tacke F Ott M Luedde T Longerich T Kubicka S Zender L"", ""year"": ""2011"", ""title"": ""Senescence surveillance of pre-malignant hepatocytes limits liver cancer development"", ""doi"": ""10.1038/nature10599"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""As part of the Reproducibility Project: Cancer Biology, we published a Registered Report ( Raouf et al., 2015 ) that described how we intended to replicate selected experiments from the paper ‘Senescence surveillance of pre-malignant hepatocytes limits liver cancer development’ ( Kang et al., 2011 )."", ""outcome"": ""uninformative"", ""outcome_quote"": ""To summarize, this replication obtained preliminary outcomes although the experiments were not continued and thus these data are unable to address whether expression of oncogenic Nras G12V in mouse livers induced cellular senescence that were dependent on a functional adaptive immunity, specifically CD4 + T cells."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly report inability to adjudicate the central finding."", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """"}"
10.1111/j.1474-9726.2010.00627.x,22bd1fb37b70c337,1,,Association of the FOXO3A locus with extreme longevity in a southern Italian centenarian study,"Anselmi CV, Malovini A, Roncarati R, Novelli V, Villa F, Condorelli G, Bellazzi R, Puca AA",2009,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": """", ""title"": ""Association of the FOXO3A locus with extreme longevity in a southern Italian centenarian study"", ""authors"": ""Anselmi CV, Malovini A, Roncarati R, Novelli V, Villa F, Condorelli G, Bellazzi R, Puca AA"", ""year"": ""2009""}, ""quote"": ""The first study published on the association of SNPs in FOXO3A (Willcox et al. 2008) was conducted on an all male population and, moreover, five of six SNPs reported by Anselmi et al. (2009) were specific for males, whereas only one was specific for females, indicating a gender effect. Moreover, adding further support to a male-specific association in the case-control study we observe no associations in females even though the sample size is larger for females than for males (about 2.5-fold)."", ""location"": ""Discussion, line 26"", ""reason"": ""Omitted target: the paper directly re-tests the tagging SNPs, haplotypes, and sex-specific associations reported by Anselmi et al. (2009) and compares its case-control results against them.""}","{""authors"": ""Anselmi CV, Malovini A, Roncarati R, Novelli V, Villa F, Condorelli G, Bellazzi R, Puca AA"", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""Association of the FOXO3A locus with extreme longevity in a southern Italian centenarian study"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""First, we conducted case-control analyses and found [as seen in previous studies (Anselmi et al."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The first study published on the association of SNPs in FOXO3A (Willcox et al. 2008) was conducted on an all male population and, moreover, five of six SNPs reported by Anselmi et al."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Agreement on the association is accompanied by an explicit contrast: no female association and/or no stronger centenarian association.""}"
10.1111/j.1474-9726.2010.00627.x,22bd1fb37b70c337,2,,Association of FOXO3A variation with human longevity confirmed in German centenarians,"Flachsbart F, Caliebe A, Kleindorp R, Blanche H, von Eller-Eberstein H, Nikolaus S, Schreiber S, Nebel A",2009,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 2, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": """", ""title"": ""Association of FOXO3A variation with human longevity confirmed in German centenarians"", ""authors"": ""Flachsbart F, Caliebe A, Kleindorp R, Blanche H, von Eller-Eberstein H, Nikolaus S, Schreiber S, Nebel A"", ""year"": ""2009""}, ""quote"": ""Considering previous reporting (Flachsbart et al. 2009) of a most significant effect in centenarians, we analyzed the centenarians separately. We were, however, not able to replicate this finding; that is, we did not find additional SNPs to be associated with longevity or an increased effect of the SNPs in the centenarians (compared to the controls) as when comparing the entire group of oldest old to the controls."", ""location"": ""Discussion, line 28"", ""reason"": ""Omitted target: the paper directly re-tests the SNP associations and centenarian-specific effect reported by Flachsbart et al. (2009).""}","{""authors"": ""Flachsbart F, Caliebe A, Kleindorp R, Blanche H, von Eller-Eberstein H, Nikolaus S, Schreiber S, Nebel A"", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""Association of FOXO3A variation with human longevity confirmed in German centenarians"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""First, we conducted case-control analyses and found [as seen in previous studies (Anselmi et al."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Considering previous reporting (Flachsbart et al. 2009) of a most significant effect in centenarians, we analyzed the centenarians separately."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Agreement on the association is accompanied by an explicit contrast: no female association and/or no stronger centenarian association.""}"
10.1111/j.1474-9726.2010.00627.x,22bd1fb37b70c337,3,,Genetic association of FOXO1A and FOXO3A with longevity trait in Han Chinese populations,"Li Y, Wang WJ, Cao H, Lu J, Wu C, Hu FY, Guo J, Zhao L, Yang F, Zhang YX, Li W, Zheng GY, Cui H, Chen X, Zhu Z, He H, Dong B, Mo X, Zeng Y, Tian XL",2009,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 3, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": """", ""title"": ""Genetic association of FOXO1A and FOXO3A with longevity trait in Han Chinese populations"", ""authors"": ""Li Y, Wang WJ, Cao H, Lu J, Wu C, Hu FY, Guo J, Zhao L, Yang F, Zhang YX, Li W, Zheng GY, Cui H, Chen X, Zhu Z, He H, Dong B, Mo X, Zeng Y, Tian XL"", ""year"": ""2009""}, ""quote"": ""Flachsbart et al. (2009) and Li et al. (2009) reported significant findings in the populations composed of both genders. However, as seen in Table 1, 2, and 3, the differences in allele, genotype and haplotype frequencies in our cohorts were found to be most pronounced for males."", ""location"": ""Discussion, line 26"", ""reason"": ""Omitted target: the paper directly re-tests the SNP, haplotype, and combined-gender associations reported by Li et al. (2009) and compares its findings against them.""}","{""authors"": ""Li Y, Wang WJ, Cao H, Lu J, Wu C, Hu FY, Guo J, Zhao L, Yang F, Zhang YX, Li W, Zheng GY, Cui H, Chen X, Zhu Z, He H, Dong B, Mo X, Zeng Y, Tian XL"", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""Genetic association of FOXO1A and FOXO3A with longevity trait in Han Chinese populations"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""First, we conducted case-control analyses and found [as seen in previous studies (Anselmi et al."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Flachsbart et al. (2009) and Li et al."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Agreement on the association is accompanied by an explicit contrast: no female association and/or no stronger centenarian association.""}"
10.1111/j.1474-9726.2010.00627.x,22bd1fb37b70c337,4,,Association of common genetic variation in the insulin ⁄ IGF1 signaling pathway with human longevity,"Pawlikowska L, Hu D, Huntsman S, Sung A, Chu C, Chen J, Joyner AH, Schork NJ, Hsueh WC, Reiner AP, Psaty BM, Atzmon G, Barzilai N, Cummings SR, Browner WS, Kwok PY, Ziv E",2009,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 4, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": """", ""title"": ""Association of common genetic variation in the insulin ⁄ IGF1 signaling pathway with human longevity"", ""authors"": ""Pawlikowska L, Hu D, Huntsman S, Sung A, Chu C, Chen J, Joyner AH, Schork NJ, Hsueh WC, Reiner AP, Psaty BM, Atzmon G, Barzilai N, Cummings SR, Browner WS, Kwok PY, Ziv E"", ""year"": ""2009""}, ""quote"": ""Four of them (rs13217795, rs2764264, rs479744, and rs9400239) have previously been reported to be associated (after correction for multiple testing) in at least one study (Flachsbart et al. 2009;Willcox et al. 2008), whereas three SNPs (rs13220810, 7762395, rs9486902) have been shown to be nominally significant (Flachsbart et al. 2009;Pawlikowska et al. 2009)."", ""location"": ""Discussion, line 26"", ""reason"": ""Omitted target: the paper directly re-tests the tagging SNP associations reported by Pawlikowska et al. (2009) and compares its allele, genotype, and sex-stratified results against them.""}","{""authors"": ""Pawlikowska L, Hu D, Huntsman S, Sung A, Chu C, Chen J, Joyner AH, Schork NJ, Hsueh WC, Reiner AP, Psaty BM, Atzmon G, Barzilai N, Cummings SR, Browner WS, Kwok PY, Ziv E"", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""Association of common genetic variation in the insulin ⁄ IGF1 signaling pathway with human longevity"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""First, we conducted case-control analyses and found [as seen in previous studies (Anselmi et al."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""No nominally significant effects (either by PCCA or PCCG) were found for the females.\nTo correct for multiple testing, while taking into account the LD between the individual SNPs, permutation analysis was performed using the Plink software."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The original association is described as female-specific; the present study finds no female associations.""}"
10.1037/pspa0000121,0ebf1107d4b56633,1,10.1177/1745691616674458,"Registered replication report: Strack, Martin, & Stepper (1988).","Wagenmakers, E.-J., Beek, T., Dijkhoff, L., Gronau, Q. F., Acosta, A., Adams, R. B., Jr.,... Zwaan, R. A.",2016,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": ""10.1177/1745691616674458"", ""title"": ""Registered replication report: Strack, Martin, & Stepper (1988)."", ""authors"": ""Wagenmakers, E.-J., Beek, T., Dijkhoff, L., Gronau, Q. F., Acosta, A., Adams, R. B., Jr.,... Zwaan, R. A."", ""year"": ""2016"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""successful""}, ""quote"": ""However, in the present work we sought to compare the outcomes of the two specific procedures that were used in past studies, one by Strack et al. (1988) and the other by Wagenmakers et al. (2016), and therefore, we repeated the same procedures that were used in those studies."", ""location"": ""Discussion, p. 660"", ""reason"": ""Omitted target: the paper explicitly repeats the camera-present procedure of Wagenmakers et al. (2016) to re-test its reported null finding and confirms the absence of the facial-feedback effect when a camera is present.""}","{""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""However, in the present work we sought to compare the outcomes of the two specific procedures that were used in past studies, one by Strack et al. (1988) and the other by Wagenmakers et al. (2016), and therefore, we repeated the same procedures that were used in those studies."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Our results suggest that the original findings of a facial feedback effect (Strack et al., 1988) and the null effect in the replication studies (Wagenmakers et al., 2016) are not contradictory: The facial-feedback effect was present when the camera was absent and participants were not concerned with performance monitoring; The effect diminished when the participants’ performance was recorded and participants were warned that their performance was being monitored."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""authors"": ""Wagenmakers, E.-J., Beek, T., Dijkhoff, L., Gronau, Q. F., Acosta, A., Adams, R. B., Jr.,... Zwaan, R. A."", ""year"": ""2016"", ""title"": ""Registered replication report: Strack, Martin, & Stepper (1988)."", ""doi"": ""10.1177/1745691616674458"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The camera condition repeats and confirms the null finding in the published replication project; this is an explicit target, not only a source of materials.""}"
10.1073/pnas.2103313118,643e1bb7a06e2a12,7,,,Fernbach; Kan; Lynch,2015,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 7, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": """", ""title"": """", ""authors"": ""Fernbach; Kan; Lynch"", ""year"": ""2015"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""mixed""}, ""quote"": ""Study 2 of Fernbach, Kan, and Lynch 2015 contains two primary predictions and analyses. This study manipulated the level of financial constraint and asked participants to provide their strategy to address the constraint."", ""location"": ""PRIMARY PROJECT REPORT: Fernbach (line 564)"", ""reason"": ""Omitted target: Fernbach, Kan, & Lynch (2015), Study 2 is one of the 20 studies replicated in O'Donnell et al. (2021), documented in the Fernbach project report, Table A, and the project spreadsheet, but omitted from the 17 shipped rows.""}","{""authors"": ""Fernbach; Kan; Lynch"", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""2"", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Study 2 of Fernbach, Kan, and Lynch 2015 contains two primary predictions and analyses."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In the replication, there was no interaction of constraint and plan type on planning mix, F(1,222) = 0.29, p = .752. In testing for response times, there was an interaction of constraint and plan time on log-transformed response time, F(2, 299) = 3.04, p = .049."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The project report compares failure of the original planning-mix finding and a significant response-time interaction. An added model matching the original fails too; no overall verdict overrides the reported partly agreeing comparisons. Full title and author initials unavailable in acquired text.""}"
