10.1073/pnas.2103313118,643e1bb7a06e2a12,14,,Evidence for opportunity cost neglect in the poor.,"Plantinga, A.; Krijnen, J. M.; Zeelenberg, M.; Breugelmans, S. M.",2018,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 14, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": """", ""title"": ""Evidence for opportunity cost neglect in the poor."", ""authors"": ""Plantinga, A.; Krijnen, J. M.; Zeelenberg, M.; Breugelmans, S. M."", ""year"": ""2018"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""successful""}, ""quote"": ""Plantinga, A., Krijnen, J. M., Zeelenberg, M., & Breugelmans, S. M. (2018). Evidence for opportunity cost neglect in the poor. Journal of Behavioral Decision Making, 31(1), 65-73."", ""location"": ""PRIMARY PROJECT REPORT: Plantinga (line 541)"", ""reason"": ""Omitted target: Plantinga, Krijnen, Zeelenberg, & Breugelmans (2018), Study 3 is one of the 20 studies replicated in O'Donnell et al. (2021), documented in the Plantinga project report, Table A, preregistration, and project spreadsheet, but omitted from the 17 shipped rows.""}","{""authors"": ""Plantinga, A.; Krijnen, J. M.; Zeelenberg, M.; Breugelmans, S. M."", ""year"": ""2018"", ""title"": ""Evidence for opportunity cost neglect in the poor."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""3"", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Plantinga, A., Krijnen, J. M., Zeelenberg, M., & Breugelmans, S. M. (2018). Evidence for opportunity cost neglect in the poor. Journal of Behavioral Decision Making, 31(1), 65-73."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The interaction was not reliable (OR: 1.02, z = 49, 95% CI [0.94, 1.11]), nor was the effect of income (OR: 1.01, z = .25, 95% CI [0.95, 1.07])."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Original central interaction is null in primary spreadsheet (r=-0.03, 95% CI [-0.11133135,0.04324818]); replication interaction is also null. Agreement is successful even without significance. Lower confidence because the narrative does not explicitly compare its null with the original null and project date labels disagree; full citation in write-up says 2018, summary says 2019.""}"
10.3390/su9071286,935c2f360e27f419,1,,Eco-Friendliness in the Brand Experience of High-Tech Products.,"Saari, U.",2016,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": """", ""title"": ""Eco-Friendliness in the Brand Experience of High-Tech Products."", ""authors"": ""Saari, U."", ""year"": ""2016"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""successful""}, ""quote"": ""In this study we replicate the extended BXS [19] cross-nationally to measure differences in the ways consumers experience the eco-friendliness of global smartphone brands in a cross-national context."", ""location"": ""Research Method and Data, line 49 (and Reference 19, line 273)"", ""reason"": ""The paper explicitly re-tests the eco-friendliness BXS extension developed in Saari (2016) [19], which had previously only been confirmed in one Western country, and confirms its construct validity and reliability across both Finland and India.""}","{""authors"": ""Saari, U."", ""year"": ""2016"", ""title"": ""Eco-Friendliness in the Brand Experience of High-Tech Products."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In this study we replicate the extended BXS [19] cross-nationally to measure differences in the ways consumers experience the eco-friendliness of global smartphone brands in a cross-national context. The survey items were selected from the previously developed and extended BXS [18,19,107]. We assess the four items measuring eco-friendliness as well as the other four brand experience dimensions from the BXS by conducting structural equation modeling (SEM) and confirmatory factor analysis (CFA) following the same steps that Brakus et al. [18] undertook."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The findings of our study indicate that the eco-friendliness dimension can be generalized in two countries with very different demographics and economic situations."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The earlier eco-friendliness dimension is explicitly retested and confirmed in both countries; changes to the other dimensions do not negate this target finding.""}"
10.3386/w20657,5125e2ec82209943,1,,Revisiting the Impacts of Teachers.,"Rothstein, Jesse.",2014,full_text,competing_supported_matches,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Rothstein, Jesse."", ""year"": ""2014"", ""title"": ""Revisiting the Impacts of Teachers."", ""doi"": """", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""statistically successful but flawed""}, ""quote"": ""However, in the same paper, Rothstein argued that the CFR methodology was invalid, given the relationship between changes in the value-added of teachers in a grade and changes in students’ prior-year test scores. We also find such a relationship, but offer a different interpretation. Rather than invalidating the CFR research design, we show that it reflects a mechanical relationship, resulting from the fact that prior test scores and the value-added estimates were sometimes estimated with the same data. In addition to replicating the CFR and Rothstein analyses, we extended their work in three ways."", ""location"": ""introduction"", ""reason"": ""The authors explicitly state that they replicate Rothstein (2014) and re-test his lagged-score placebo finding in Table 5, reproducing the statistically significant relationship (.268) while showing it is mechanically confounded by using overlapping data to estimate the dependent and independent variables.""}","{""authors"": ""Rothstein, Jesse."", ""year"": ""2014"", ""title"": ""Revisiting the Impacts of Teachers."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In addition to replicating the CFR and Rothstein analyses, we extended their work in three ways."", ""outcome"": ""statistically successful but flawed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In a recent paper, Rothstein (2014) reports a statistically significant relationship between change in teacher value-added and changes in baseline achievement as prima facie evidence of bias in the CFR method. We find similar results, but offer a different interpretation. We find that the predicted change has a coefficient of .268 when lagged scores are the dependent variable. However, rather than invalidating the CFR methodology, the lagged score test merely demonstrates the hazards of using the same data to estimate the dependent and 19 independent variables."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Statistical placebo relationship is replicated but shown to be mechanically confounded, a central methodological critique.""}"
10.1037/a0028072,137a8b84a4506060,1,10.1080/01690968508402073,Lexical acquisition and the modular lexicon.,"Forster, K. I.",1985,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Forster, K. I."", ""year"": ""1985"", ""title"": ""Lexical acquisition and the modular lexicon."", ""doi"": ""10.1080/01690968508402073"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""3"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """"}, ""quote"": ""In this final experiment, a more powerful test of the episodic L2 hypothesis is carried out using a more robust form of priming, namely, repetition priming rather than translation priming. As explained earlier, it has been found that in an episodic recognition task, old words show strong masked repetition priming, but there is no priming for new words (Forster, 1985; Rajaram & Neely, 1992). This result was obtained with native English speakers, so the priming is L1–L1."", ""location"": ""Experiment 3 introduction, p. 8, lines 1701-1714"", ""reason"": ""Experiment 3 genuinely re-tests Forster's (1985) specific empirical finding—that in an episodic recognition task with native English speakers, studied ('old') words show strong masked repetition priming whereas unstudied ('new') words show no priming—by testing a new sample of 24 native English speakers alongside 20 Chinese–English bilinguals on old versus new English words in an episodic recognition task with masked repetition priming, and confirms the original finding in the native English speaker sample (significant priming for old items, M = 48 ms, p = .0001, and no significant priming for new items, M = 4 ms, p = .28; explicitly compared with Forster, 1985 in the Experiment 3 Discussion, lines 1969-1977).""}","{""authors"": ""Forster, K. I."", ""year"": ""1985"", ""title"": ""Lexical acquisition and the modular lexicon."", ""doi"": ""10.1080/01690968508402073"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""In this final experiment, a more powerful test of the episodic L2 hypothesis is carried out using a more robust form of priming, namely, repetition priming rather than translation priming. As explained earlier, it has been found that in an episodic recognition task, old words show strong masked repetition priming, but there is no priming for new words (Forster, 1985; Rajaram & Neely, 1992)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The findings are clear. In an episodic recognition task, Chinese– English bilinguals show repetition priming for both old and new English (L2) words. This contrasts sharply with the results for the monolingual English speakers, where repetition priming was ob- tained for old words only."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.1037/a0028072,137a8b84a4506060,2,10.1016/0749-596X(92)90009-M,Dissociative masked repetition priming and word frequency effects in lexical decision and episodic recognition tasks.,"Rajaram, S., & Neely, J. H.",1992,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 2, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Rajaram, S., & Neely, J. H."", ""year"": ""1992"", ""title"": ""Dissociative masked repetition priming and word frequency effects in lexical decision and episodic recognition tasks."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/0749-596X(92)90009-M"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""3"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """"}, ""quote"": ""In this final experiment, a more powerful test of the episodic L2 hypothesis is carried out using a more robust form of priming, namely, repetition priming rather than translation priming. As explained earlier, it has been found that in an episodic recognition task, old words show strong masked repetition priming, but there is no priming for new words (Forster, 1985; Rajaram & Neely, 1992). This result was obtained with native English speakers, so the priming is L1–L1."", ""location"": ""Experiment 3 introduction, p. 8, lines 1701-1714"", ""reason"": ""Experiment 3 genuinely re-tests Rajaram and Neely's (1992) specific empirical finding in episodic recognition—that native English speakers show masked repetition priming only for studied ('old') words and not for unstudied ('new') words—by collecting new data from 24 native English speakers alongside 20 Chinese–English bilinguals in an episodic recognition task with masked repetition priming, and successfully replicates that pattern in the native English speaker sample (priming for old items only, M = 48 ms, p = .0001, vs. new items, M = 4 ms, p = .28; explicitly compared with Rajaram & Neely, 1992 in the Experiment 3 Discussion, lines 1969-1977).""}","{""authors"": ""Rajaram, S., & Neely, J. H."", ""year"": ""1992"", ""title"": ""Dissociative masked repetition priming and word frequency effects in lexical decision and episodic recognition tasks."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/0749-596X(92)90009-M"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""In this final experiment, a more powerful test of the episodic L2 hypothesis is carried out using a more robust form of priming, namely, repetition priming rather than translation priming. As explained earlier, it has been found that in an episodic recognition task, old words show strong masked repetition priming, but there is no priming for new words (Forster, 1985; Rajaram & Neely, 1992)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The findings are clear. In an episodic recognition task, Chinese– English bilinguals show repetition priming for both old and new English (L2) words. This contrasts sharply with the results for the monolingual English speakers, where repetition priming was ob- tained for old words only."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.1002/jaba.921,fadd7348ab03f217,1,10.1002/jaba.863,Machine learning to analyze single-case graphs: A comparison to visual inspection.,"Lanovaz, M. J., & Hranchuk, K.",2021,full_text,,error,obvious,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""obvious"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Lanovaz, M. J., & Hranchuk, K."", ""year"": ""2021"", ""title"": ""Machine learning to analyze single-case graphs: A comparison to visual inspection."", ""doi"": ""10.1002/jaba.863"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined""}, ""quote"": ""A secondary purpose was to replicate and extend Lanovaz and Hranchuk (2021) by examining correspondence between visual inspection, the conservative dual-criteria method, and the machine-learning algorithms on a novel dataset. We extended both studies by using clinical data and adding a 10-point scale with definitional criteria to visual-inspection ratings."", ""location"": ""Introduction"", ""reason"": ""The authors explicitly define a secondary purpose as replicating Lanovaz and Hranchuk (2021) on new clinical data, distinct from the primary Wolfe et al. (2018) comparison. The reference identifies DOI 10.1002/jaba.863; the sole shipped pair is Wolfe (10.1002/jaba.453). Parent reviewed the cited results/purpose and reference identity against all supplied shipped originals. This decision confirms the missing paper-level pair only; its overall outcome has not been adjudicated.""}","{""authors"": ""Lanovaz, M. J., & Hranchuk, K."", ""year"": ""2021"", ""title"": ""Machine learning to analyze single-case graphs: A comparison to visual inspection."", ""doi"": ""10.1002/jaba.863"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""A secondary purpose was to replicate and extend Lanovaz and Hranchuk (2021) by examining correspondence between visual inspection, the conservative dual-criteria method, and the machine-learning algorithms on a novel dataset."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In contrast, raters performed better on the clinical graphs than previously reported by Lanovaz and Hranchuk (2021) on simulated ones."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The discussion explicitly contrasts improved rater performance and the phase-length relation with this target; the predicted classifier agreement advantage was absent. No overall author verdict overrides these differences.""}"
10.1017/s0261444825100992,c93427e18d97d673,1,10.1177/1362168820938814,Commitment to the profession of ELT and an organization: A profile of expat faculty in South Korea.,"Moodie, I.",2023,full_text,,error,obvious,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""obvious"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Moodie, I."", ""year"": ""2023"", ""title"": ""Commitment to the profession of ELT and an organization: A profile of expat faculty in South Korea."", ""doi"": ""10.1177/1362168820938814"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined""}, ""quote"": ""Similarly, based on a prior study (Moodie, 2023), Hypothesis 6 expected higher affective and normative commitment among NESTs with formal teacher qualifications. These results did not replicate, indicating that teacher qualifications do not necessarily translate into stronger commitment profiles among expatriate NESTs."", ""location"": ""Discussion"", ""reason"": ""Hypothesis 6 directly retests Moodie (2023)'s teacher-qualification commitment finding and the discussion states that these results did not replicate. The reference identifies DOI 10.1177/1362168820938814. The sole shipped pair is McInerney et al. (2015), a different paper. Parent reviewed the cited results/purpose and reference identity against all supplied shipped originals. This decision confirms the missing paper-level pair only; its overall outcome has not been adjudicated.""}","{""authors"": ""Moodie, I."", ""year"": ""2023"", ""title"": ""Commitment to the profession of ELT and an organization: A profile of expat faculty in South Korea."", ""doi"": ""10.1177/1362168820938814"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Similarly, based on a prior study (Moodie, 2023), Hypothesis 6 expected higher affective and normative commitment among NESTs with formal teacher qualifications. These results did not replicate, indicating that teacher qualifications do not necessarily translate into stronger commitment profiles among expatriate NESTs."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Similarly, based on a prior study (Moodie, 2023), Hypothesis 6 expected higher affective and normative commitment among NESTs with formal teacher qualifications. These results did not replicate, indicating that teacher qualifications do not necessarily translate into stronger commitment profiles among expatriate NESTs."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The extension explicitly re-tests the published qualification–commitment result and reports it did not replicate.""}"
10.1177/0022022110381114,3531eb735322f77d,3,,Development of self-recognition in the infant.,"Bertenthal, B., & Fisher, K.",1978,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 3, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Bertenthal, B., & Fisher, K."", ""year"": ""1978"", ""title"": ""Development of self-recognition in the infant."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""mixed""}, ""quote"": ""Numerous developmental studies of predominantly urban Western middle-class children suggest that children reach this milestone by 18 to 24 months of age. By 18 months, approximately 50% pass and by 24 months a significant majority of children (more than 70%) are reported to self- refer by touching or removing a mark from their own body (face or other body region) while exploring their mirror image (Amsterdam, 1972; Asendorpf, Warkentin, & Baudonnière, 1996; Bard, Todd, Bernier, Love, & Leavens, 2006; Bertenthal & Fisher, 1978; Lewis & Brooks-Gunn, 1979; Lewis & Ramsay, 2004; Nielsen, Dissanayake, & Kashima, 2003; Schulman & Kaplowitz, 1976). Here we report cross-cultural observations on the emergence of mirror self-recognition (MSR), using a simplified mark test, raising questions regarding the meaning and universal validity of this test as an ontogenetic benchmark of self-concept."", ""location"": ""introduction, p. 2"", ""reason"": ""Bertenthal and Fisher (1978) is attributed not only in the introduction's 18-to-24-month Western benchmark set that the paper attempts to replicate across cultures, but also in the Method and General Discussion sections as a direct source of the >60% (60% to 85%) pass-rate benchmark tested via the binomial analysis and contrasted with the cross-cultural results. Rejecting it in favor of Amsterdam (1972) confuses Amsterdam's role as the original paradigm source with the empirical benchmark findings directly re-tested here.""}","{""authors"": ""Bertenthal, B., & Fisher, K."", ""year"": ""1978"", ""title"": ""Development of self-recognition in the infant."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Numerous developmental studies of predominantly urban Western middle-class children suggest that children reach this milestone by 18 to 24 months of age. By 18 months, approximately 50% pass and by 24 months a significant majority of children (more than 70%) are reported to self- refer by touching or removing a mark from their own body (face or other body region) while exploring their mirror image (Amsterdam, 1972; Asendorpf, Warkentin, & Baudonnière, 1996; Bard, Todd, Bernier, Love, & Leavens, 2006; Bertenthal & Fisher, 1978; Lewis & Brooks-Gunn, 1979; Lewis & Ramsay, 2004; Nielsen, Dissanayake, & Kashima, 2003; Schulman & Kaplowitz, 1976). Here we report cross-cultural observations on the emergence of mirror self-recognition (MSR), using a simplified mark test, raising questions regarding the meaning and universal validity of this test as an ontogenetic benchmark of self-concept. The basic rationale behind the mark test is that when a child is marked surreptitiously and attempts to touch the mark on the body, as opposed to doing nothing or trying to reach for it on the mirror surface, it indicates that the specular image stands for the child’s own body. Self-oriented gestures are taken as the behavioral index of self-recognition, indicating that the embodied self is the referent of what is seen in the mirror (Rochat, 2003). In a different account, Nielsen, Suddendorf, and Slaughter (2006) claim that the mark test is passed by individuals who have a rapidly updatable image of themselves that they recognize in the mirror. Regardless of the rela- tive validity of these accounts, the general consensus is that passing the mark test indicates that children recognize their image and that those who fail the test do not. From a comparative perspective, chimpanzees, orangutans, dolphins, and Asian elephants are among the few nonhuman species reported to pass the mark test (Gallup, 1982; Plotnik & de Waal, 2006; Povinelli, 1995; Reiss & Marino, 1998). Nonhuman individuals passing the test typically have extensive prior experience with reflective surfaces. In contrast, research with humans suggests that children’s relative familiarity with mirrors, which greatly varies across contexts, does not correlate with the age at which the mark test is passed (Priel & deSchonen, 1986). Priel and deShonen (1986) tested Bedouin nomadic children with no previous mirror experience and compared them to same-age Israeli children familiar with mirrors. They found no significant difference in the developmental onset of MSR between mirror familiar and unfa- miliar children. However, more recent cross-cultural studies point to significant cultural variations in the onset of MSR. Keller and collaborators compared 18- to 20-month-olds from urban Greece, Costa Rica, Germany, as well as from a rural community in Cameroon, and they report a greater proportion of German, Greek, and Costa Rican children passing the test (more than 50%), compared to Cameroonian children (less than 4%) (Keller, Kartner, Borke, Yovsi, & Kleis, 2005; Keller et al., 2004). These authors correlate such variations to variations in par- enting strategies that exist across these cultures, fostering more or less autonomy in the young child. If such variability is validated with other cultures, testing children older than 18 to 20 months would allow us to determine whether this variability pertains only to a developmental delay, pos- sibly resulting from variations in parenting styles (see Keller et al., 2005; Keller et al., 2004) or whether such differences persist throughout development. If the latter is the case, this would sug- gest that there may be culture-specific responses to the passing of the mirror mark test (i.e., greater occurrence of potential false negatives). To probe this alternative explanation, we per- formed two experiments testing (a) whether cross-cultural variability in the passing of the mirror mark test could be verified across more cultures and (b) whether such variability would persist beyond the age at which children typically are reported to pass the mirror mark test. In two experiments, we tested children 18 to 72 months old living in seven distinct cultural groups. Because of the exploratory nature of this work, aside from probing these questions, no specific hypotheses were made."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The second experiment demonstrates that a significant amount of variability exists across cultural contexts in children’s behavioral response to this version of the MSR test that measures spontaneous self-recognition (see also Lewis & Ramsey, 2004). Consistent with past research, 77% to 88% of Western children (Canada and United States) pass this version of the MSR test by spontaneously displaying self-oriented behaviors. In contrast, only half of the children from Saint Lucia, Grenada, or Peru and none of the Fijian children of the same age showed any sign of such behaviors."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Western samples agree with the cited developmental benchmark; non-Western samples explicitly contrast with it. No overall replication verdict supersedes these comparisons.""}"
10.1177/0022022110381114,3531eb735322f77d,4,,Social cognition and the acquisition of self.,"Lewis, M., & Brooks-Gunn, J.",1979,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 4, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Lewis, M., & Brooks-Gunn, J."", ""year"": ""1979"", ""title"": ""Social cognition and the acquisition of self."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""mixed""}, ""quote"": ""Numerous developmental studies of predominantly urban Western middle-class children suggest that children reach this milestone by 18 to 24 months of age. By 18 months, approximately 50% pass and by 24 months a significant majority of children (more than 70%) are reported to self- refer by touching or removing a mark from their own body (face or other body region) while exploring their mirror image (Amsterdam, 1972; Asendorpf, Warkentin, & Baudonnière, 1996; Bard, Todd, Bernier, Love, & Leavens, 2006; Bertenthal & Fisher, 1978; Lewis & Brooks-Gunn, 1979; Lewis & Ramsay, 2004; Nielsen, Dissanayake, & Kashima, 2003; Schulman & Kaplowitz, 1976). Here we report cross-cultural observations on the emergence of mirror self-recognition (MSR), using a simplified mark test, raising questions regarding the meaning and universal validity of this test as an ontogenetic benchmark of self-concept."", ""location"": ""introduction, p. 2"", ""reason"": ""Lewis and Brooks-Gunn (1979) is directly cited for the 18-to-24-month Western empirical benchmark in the introduction, for the .60 binomial test threshold in the Method section, and as an explicit comparison in both the Experiment 1 results and General Discussion. Its reported developmental finding is re-tested on the same footing as Amsterdam (1972), which is distinguished only as the source of the original linguistic mark-test procedure.""}","{""authors"": ""Lewis, M., & Brooks-Gunn, J."", ""year"": ""1979"", ""title"": ""Social cognition and the acquisition of self."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Numerous developmental studies of predominantly urban Western middle-class children suggest that children reach this milestone by 18 to 24 months of age. By 18 months, approximately 50% pass and by 24 months a significant majority of children (more than 70%) are reported to self- refer by touching or removing a mark from their own body (face or other body region) while exploring their mirror image (Amsterdam, 1972; Asendorpf, Warkentin, & Baudonnière, 1996; Bard, Todd, Bernier, Love, & Leavens, 2006; Bertenthal & Fisher, 1978; Lewis & Brooks-Gunn, 1979; Lewis & Ramsay, 2004; Nielsen, Dissanayake, & Kashima, 2003; Schulman & Kaplowitz, 1976). Here we report cross-cultural observations on the emergence of mirror self-recognition (MSR), using a simplified mark test, raising questions regarding the meaning and universal validity of this test as an ontogenetic benchmark of self-concept. The basic rationale behind the mark test is that when a child is marked surreptitiously and attempts to touch the mark on the body, as opposed to doing nothing or trying to reach for it on the mirror surface, it indicates that the specular image stands for the child’s own body. Self-oriented gestures are taken as the behavioral index of self-recognition, indicating that the embodied self is the referent of what is seen in the mirror (Rochat, 2003). In a different account, Nielsen, Suddendorf, and Slaughter (2006) claim that the mark test is passed by individuals who have a rapidly updatable image of themselves that they recognize in the mirror. Regardless of the rela- tive validity of these accounts, the general consensus is that passing the mark test indicates that children recognize their image and that those who fail the test do not. From a comparative perspective, chimpanzees, orangutans, dolphins, and Asian elephants are among the few nonhuman species reported to pass the mark test (Gallup, 1982; Plotnik & de Waal, 2006; Povinelli, 1995; Reiss & Marino, 1998). Nonhuman individuals passing the test typically have extensive prior experience with reflective surfaces. In contrast, research with humans suggests that children’s relative familiarity with mirrors, which greatly varies across contexts, does not correlate with the age at which the mark test is passed (Priel & deSchonen, 1986). Priel and deShonen (1986) tested Bedouin nomadic children with no previous mirror experience and compared them to same-age Israeli children familiar with mirrors. They found no significant difference in the developmental onset of MSR between mirror familiar and unfa- miliar children. However, more recent cross-cultural studies point to significant cultural variations in the onset of MSR. Keller and collaborators compared 18- to 20-month-olds from urban Greece, Costa Rica, Germany, as well as from a rural community in Cameroon, and they report a greater proportion of German, Greek, and Costa Rican children passing the test (more than 50%), compared to Cameroonian children (less than 4%) (Keller, Kartner, Borke, Yovsi, & Kleis, 2005; Keller et al., 2004). These authors correlate such variations to variations in par- enting strategies that exist across these cultures, fostering more or less autonomy in the young child. If such variability is validated with other cultures, testing children older than 18 to 20 months would allow us to determine whether this variability pertains only to a developmental delay, pos- sibly resulting from variations in parenting styles (see Keller et al., 2005; Keller et al., 2004) or whether such differences persist throughout development. If the latter is the case, this would sug- gest that there may be culture-specific responses to the passing of the mirror mark test (i.e., greater occurrence of potential false negatives). To probe this alternative explanation, we per- formed two experiments testing (a) whether cross-cultural variability in the passing of the mirror mark test could be verified across more cultures and (b) whether such variability would persist beyond the age at which children typically are reported to pass the mirror mark test. In two experiments, we tested children 18 to 72 months old living in seven distinct cultural groups. Because of the exploratory nature of this work, aside from probing these questions, no specific hypotheses were made."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The second experiment demonstrates that a significant amount of variability exists across cultural contexts in children’s behavioral response to this version of the MSR test that measures spontaneous self-recognition (see also Lewis & Ramsey, 2004). Consistent with past research, 77% to 88% of Western children (Canada and United States) pass this version of the MSR test by spontaneously displaying self-oriented behaviors. In contrast, only half of the children from Saint Lucia, Grenada, or Peru and none of the Fijian children of the same age showed any sign of such behaviors."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Western samples agree with the cited developmental benchmark; non-Western samples explicitly contrast with it. No overall replication verdict supersedes these comparisons.""}"
10.1177/0022022110381114,3531eb735322f77d,5,,"Development of self-recognition, personal pronoun use, and pretend play during the 2nd year.","Lewis, M., & Ramsay, D.",2004,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 5, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Lewis, M., & Ramsay, D."", ""year"": ""2004"", ""title"": ""Development of self-recognition, personal pronoun use, and pretend play during the 2nd year."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""mixed""}, ""quote"": ""Numerous developmental studies of predominantly urban Western middle-class children suggest that children reach this milestone by 18 to 24 months of age. By 18 months, approximately 50% pass and by 24 months a significant majority of children (more than 70%) are reported to self- refer by touching or removing a mark from their own body (face or other body region) while exploring their mirror image (Amsterdam, 1972; Asendorpf, Warkentin, & Baudonnière, 1996; Bard, Todd, Bernier, Love, & Leavens, 2006; Bertenthal & Fisher, 1978; Lewis & Brooks-Gunn, 1979; Lewis & Ramsay, 2004; Nielsen, Dissanayake, & Kashima, 2003; Schulman & Kaplowitz, 1976). Here we report cross-cultural observations on the emergence of mirror self-recognition (MSR), using a simplified mark test, raising questions regarding the meaning and universal validity of this test as an ontogenetic benchmark of self-concept."", ""location"": ""introduction, p. 2"", ""reason"": ""Lewis and Ramsay (2004) is cited in the introduction as part of the attributed 18-to-24-month Western empirical benchmark and is explicitly compared against the new cross-cultural data in both Experiment 1 (noting its >80% pass rate by 21 months and 100% by 24 months using a comparable procedure) and the General Discussion of Experiment 2. The study directly re-tests this empirical benchmark rather than merely citing it as background while testing the Amsterdam (1972) paradigm source alone.""}","{""authors"": ""Lewis, M., & Ramsay, D."", ""year"": ""2004"", ""title"": ""Development of self-recognition, personal pronoun use, and pretend play during the 2nd year."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Numerous developmental studies of predominantly urban Western middle-class children suggest that children reach this milestone by 18 to 24 months of age. By 18 months, approximately 50% pass and by 24 months a significant majority of children (more than 70%) are reported to self- refer by touching or removing a mark from their own body (face or other body region) while exploring their mirror image (Amsterdam, 1972; Asendorpf, Warkentin, & Baudonnière, 1996; Bard, Todd, Bernier, Love, & Leavens, 2006; Bertenthal & Fisher, 1978; Lewis & Brooks-Gunn, 1979; Lewis & Ramsay, 2004; Nielsen, Dissanayake, & Kashima, 2003; Schulman & Kaplowitz, 1976). Here we report cross-cultural observations on the emergence of mirror self-recognition (MSR), using a simplified mark test, raising questions regarding the meaning and universal validity of this test as an ontogenetic benchmark of self-concept. The basic rationale behind the mark test is that when a child is marked surreptitiously and attempts to touch the mark on the body, as opposed to doing nothing or trying to reach for it on the mirror surface, it indicates that the specular image stands for the child’s own body. Self-oriented gestures are taken as the behavioral index of self-recognition, indicating that the embodied self is the referent of what is seen in the mirror (Rochat, 2003). In a different account, Nielsen, Suddendorf, and Slaughter (2006) claim that the mark test is passed by individuals who have a rapidly updatable image of themselves that they recognize in the mirror. Regardless of the rela- tive validity of these accounts, the general consensus is that passing the mark test indicates that children recognize their image and that those who fail the test do not. From a comparative perspective, chimpanzees, orangutans, dolphins, and Asian elephants are among the few nonhuman species reported to pass the mark test (Gallup, 1982; Plotnik & de Waal, 2006; Povinelli, 1995; Reiss & Marino, 1998). Nonhuman individuals passing the test typically have extensive prior experience with reflective surfaces. In contrast, research with humans suggests that children’s relative familiarity with mirrors, which greatly varies across contexts, does not correlate with the age at which the mark test is passed (Priel & deSchonen, 1986). Priel and deShonen (1986) tested Bedouin nomadic children with no previous mirror experience and compared them to same-age Israeli children familiar with mirrors. They found no significant difference in the developmental onset of MSR between mirror familiar and unfa- miliar children. However, more recent cross-cultural studies point to significant cultural variations in the onset of MSR. Keller and collaborators compared 18- to 20-month-olds from urban Greece, Costa Rica, Germany, as well as from a rural community in Cameroon, and they report a greater proportion of German, Greek, and Costa Rican children passing the test (more than 50%), compared to Cameroonian children (less than 4%) (Keller, Kartner, Borke, Yovsi, & Kleis, 2005; Keller et al., 2004). These authors correlate such variations to variations in par- enting strategies that exist across these cultures, fostering more or less autonomy in the young child. If such variability is validated with other cultures, testing children older than 18 to 20 months would allow us to determine whether this variability pertains only to a developmental delay, pos- sibly resulting from variations in parenting styles (see Keller et al., 2005; Keller et al., 2004) or whether such differences persist throughout development. If the latter is the case, this would sug- gest that there may be culture-specific responses to the passing of the mirror mark test (i.e., greater occurrence of potential false negatives). To probe this alternative explanation, we per- formed two experiments testing (a) whether cross-cultural variability in the passing of the mirror mark test could be verified across more cultures and (b) whether such variability would persist beyond the age at which children typically are reported to pass the mirror mark test. In two experiments, we tested children 18 to 72 months old living in seven distinct cultural groups. Because of the exploratory nature of this work, aside from probing these questions, no specific hypotheses were made."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The second experiment demonstrates that a significant amount of variability exists across cultural contexts in children’s behavioral response to this version of the MSR test that measures spontaneous self-recognition (see also Lewis & Ramsey, 2004). Consistent with past research, 77% to 88% of Western children (Canada and United States) pass this version of the MSR test by spontaneously displaying self-oriented behaviors. In contrast, only half of the children from Saint Lucia, Grenada, or Peru and none of the Fijian children of the same age showed any sign of such behaviors."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Western samples agree with the cited developmental benchmark; non-Western samples explicitly contrast with it. No overall replication verdict supersedes these comparisons.""}"
10.1177/0022022110381114,3531eb735322f77d,6,,A longitudinal investigation of self-other discrimination and the emergence of mirror self-recognition.,"Nielsen, M., Dissanayake, C., & Kashima, Y.",2003,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 6, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Nielsen, M., Dissanayake, C., & Kashima, Y."", ""year"": ""2003"", ""title"": ""A longitudinal investigation of self-other discrimination and the emergence of mirror self-recognition."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""mixed""}, ""quote"": ""Numerous developmental studies of predominantly urban Western middle-class children suggest that children reach this milestone by 18 to 24 months of age. By 18 months, approximately 50% pass and by 24 months a significant majority of children (more than 70%) are reported to self- refer by touching or removing a mark from their own body (face or other body region) while exploring their mirror image (Amsterdam, 1972; Asendorpf, Warkentin, & Baudonnière, 1996; Bard, Todd, Bernier, Love, & Leavens, 2006; Bertenthal & Fisher, 1978; Lewis & Brooks-Gunn, 1979; Lewis & Ramsay, 2004; Nielsen, Dissanayake, & Kashima, 2003; Schulman & Kaplowitz, 1976). Here we report cross-cultural observations on the emergence of mirror self-recognition (MSR), using a simplified mark test, raising questions regarding the meaning and universal validity of this test as an ontogenetic benchmark of self-concept."", ""location"": ""introduction, p. 2"", ""reason"": ""Nielsen, Dissanayake, and Kashima (2003) is both attributed in the introduction for the specific 18-to-24-month Western pass-rate finding that the paper attempts to replicate across cultures and cited in the Method section as one of the Western child studies using the simplified nonlinguistic mark-test procedure adopted here. It is therefore a directly re-tested empirical benchmark rather than a method-only or generic background citation.""}","{""authors"": ""Nielsen, M., Dissanayake, C., & Kashima, Y."", ""year"": ""2003"", ""title"": ""A longitudinal investigation of self-other discrimination and the emergence of mirror self-recognition."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Numerous developmental studies of predominantly urban Western middle-class children suggest that children reach this milestone by 18 to 24 months of age. By 18 months, approximately 50% pass and by 24 months a significant majority of children (more than 70%) are reported to self- refer by touching or removing a mark from their own body (face or other body region) while exploring their mirror image (Amsterdam, 1972; Asendorpf, Warkentin, & Baudonnière, 1996; Bard, Todd, Bernier, Love, & Leavens, 2006; Bertenthal & Fisher, 1978; Lewis & Brooks-Gunn, 1979; Lewis & Ramsay, 2004; Nielsen, Dissanayake, & Kashima, 2003; Schulman & Kaplowitz, 1976). Here we report cross-cultural observations on the emergence of mirror self-recognition (MSR), using a simplified mark test, raising questions regarding the meaning and universal validity of this test as an ontogenetic benchmark of self-concept. The basic rationale behind the mark test is that when a child is marked surreptitiously and attempts to touch the mark on the body, as opposed to doing nothing or trying to reach for it on the mirror surface, it indicates that the specular image stands for the child’s own body. Self-oriented gestures are taken as the behavioral index of self-recognition, indicating that the embodied self is the referent of what is seen in the mirror (Rochat, 2003). In a different account, Nielsen, Suddendorf, and Slaughter (2006) claim that the mark test is passed by individuals who have a rapidly updatable image of themselves that they recognize in the mirror. Regardless of the rela- tive validity of these accounts, the general consensus is that passing the mark test indicates that children recognize their image and that those who fail the test do not. From a comparative perspective, chimpanzees, orangutans, dolphins, and Asian elephants are among the few nonhuman species reported to pass the mark test (Gallup, 1982; Plotnik & de Waal, 2006; Povinelli, 1995; Reiss & Marino, 1998). Nonhuman individuals passing the test typically have extensive prior experience with reflective surfaces. In contrast, research with humans suggests that children’s relative familiarity with mirrors, which greatly varies across contexts, does not correlate with the age at which the mark test is passed (Priel & deSchonen, 1986). Priel and deShonen (1986) tested Bedouin nomadic children with no previous mirror experience and compared them to same-age Israeli children familiar with mirrors. They found no significant difference in the developmental onset of MSR between mirror familiar and unfa- miliar children. However, more recent cross-cultural studies point to significant cultural variations in the onset of MSR. Keller and collaborators compared 18- to 20-month-olds from urban Greece, Costa Rica, Germany, as well as from a rural community in Cameroon, and they report a greater proportion of German, Greek, and Costa Rican children passing the test (more than 50%), compared to Cameroonian children (less than 4%) (Keller, Kartner, Borke, Yovsi, & Kleis, 2005; Keller et al., 2004). These authors correlate such variations to variations in par- enting strategies that exist across these cultures, fostering more or less autonomy in the young child. If such variability is validated with other cultures, testing children older than 18 to 20 months would allow us to determine whether this variability pertains only to a developmental delay, pos- sibly resulting from variations in parenting styles (see Keller et al., 2005; Keller et al., 2004) or whether such differences persist throughout development. If the latter is the case, this would sug- gest that there may be culture-specific responses to the passing of the mirror mark test (i.e., greater occurrence of potential false negatives). To probe this alternative explanation, we per- formed two experiments testing (a) whether cross-cultural variability in the passing of the mirror mark test could be verified across more cultures and (b) whether such variability would persist beyond the age at which children typically are reported to pass the mirror mark test. In two experiments, we tested children 18 to 72 months old living in seven distinct cultural groups. Because of the exploratory nature of this work, aside from probing these questions, no specific hypotheses were made."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The second experiment demonstrates that a significant amount of variability exists across cultural contexts in children’s behavioral response to this version of the MSR test that measures spontaneous self-recognition (see also Lewis & Ramsey, 2004). Consistent with past research, 77% to 88% of Western children (Canada and United States) pass this version of the MSR test by spontaneously displaying self-oriented behaviors. In contrast, only half of the children from Saint Lucia, Grenada, or Peru and none of the Fijian children of the same age showed any sign of such behaviors."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Western samples agree with the cited developmental benchmark; non-Western samples explicitly contrast with it. No overall replication verdict supersedes these comparisons.""}"
10.1037/a0025169,da9c8f0984382fe0,1,10.1016/j.jrp.2005.05.003,The causal effects of extraversion on positive affect and neuroticism on negative affect: Manipulating state extraversion and state neuroticism in an experimental approach.,"McNiel, J. M., & Fleeson, W.",2006,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""McNiel, J. M., & Fleeson, W."", ""year"": ""2006"", ""title"": ""The causal effects of extraversion on positive affect and neuroticism on negative affect: Manipulating state extraversion and state neuroticism in an experimental approach"", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.jrp.2005.05.003"", ""authors_o"": ""McNiel; Fleeson"", ""year_o"": ""2006"", ""title_o"": ""The causal effects of extraversion on positive affect and neuroticism on negative affect: Manipulating state extraversion and state neuroticism in an experimental approach"", ""doi_o"": ""10.1016/j.jrp.2005.05.003"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Although possible, two studies have assessed negative affect under similar circumstances and have failed to find the state by trait interaction that would suggest a mood cost for acting counterdispositionally (McNiel & Fleeson, 2006; McNiel et al., 2010). On the other hand, one of these samples was small (n⫽45) and both employed a within-subject design that could promote demand or carryover effects. In fact, the studies did find significant order effects and some inconsistent trait and acting condition effects on negative affect. So although there was not a significant interaction suggesting an affective cost, the full mood consequences of acting more or less extraverted require some clarification, particularly with regard to negative affect. Therefore, our studies include between-subjects manipulations, larger sample sizes, and measures of both positive and negative affect to better assess any possible distress that might accompany counterdispositional behavior."", ""outcome_quote"": ""Results replicated and extended past findings suggesting that acting extraverted produces hedonic benefits regardless of disposition. Positive affect increased and negative affect did not, even for participants acting out of character."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true}, ""quote"": ""Although possible, two studies have assessed negative affect under similar circumstances and have failed to find the state by trait interaction that would suggest a mood cost for acting counterdispositionally (McNiel & Fleeson, 2006; McNiel et al., 2010). On the other hand, one of these samples was small (n⫽45) and both employed a within-subject design that could promote demand or carryover effects. In fact, the studies did find significant order effects and some inconsistent trait and acting condition effects on negative affect. So although there was not a significant interaction suggesting an affective cost, the full mood consequences of acting more or less extraverted require some clarification, particularly with regard to negative affect. Therefore, our studies include between-subjects manipulations, larger sample sizes, and measures of both positive and negative affect to better assess any possible distress that might accompany counterdispositional behavior."", ""location"": ""introduction"", ""reason"": ""The paper directly re-tests McNiel and Fleeson's (2006) empirical finding that manipulating state extraversion/introversion produces no state-by-trait interaction on negative affect (and increases positive affect regardless of disposition), explicitly critiquing the small sample size and within-subject design of the two prior negative-affect studies and conducting a stronger between-subjects test across Studies 1 and 2 that confirms the absence of a counterdispositional negative-affect cost (successful replication).""}","{""authors"": ""McNiel, J. M., & Fleeson, W."", ""year"": ""2006"", ""title"": ""The causal effects of extraversion on positive affect and neuroticism on negative affect: Manipulating state extraversion and state neuroticism in an experimental approach."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.jrp.2005.05.003"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Although possible, two studies have assessed negative affect under similar circumstances and have failed to find the state by trait interaction that would suggest a mood cost for acting counterdispositionally (McNiel & Fleeson, 2006; McNiel et al., 2010). Therefore, our studies include between-subjects manipulations, larger sample sizes, and measures of both positive and negative affect to better assess any possible distress that might accompany counterdispositional behavior."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""This study tested whether the pattern of relationships among extraversion, mood, behavior, and cognition found in Study 1 would generalize to a new interview situation. Very similar to previous research, participants reported more positive affect when acting extraverted, and this did not depend on their dispositional levels of extraversion. We again tested whether acting counterdispositionally might cause negative affect (along with positive affect in the case of introverts) and again failed to find any evidence for this idea."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The reported positive-affect benefit and/or absence of dispositional negative-affect cost agrees with this original’s findings. Cognitive depletion is a novel extension, not a failed replication of these mood findings.""}"
