donic benefits regardless of disposition. Positive affect increased and negative affect did not, even for participants acting out of character."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true}, ""quote"": ""Although possible, two studies have assessed negative affect under similar circumstances and have failed to find the state by trait interaction that would suggest a mood cost for acting counterdispositionally (McNiel & Fleeson, 2006; McNiel et al., 2010). On the other hand, one of these samples was small (n⫽45) and both employed a within-subject design that could promote demand or carryover effects. In fact, the studies did find significant order effects and some inconsistent trait and acting condition effects on negative affect. So although there was not a significant interaction suggesting an affective cost, the full mood consequences of acting more or less extraverted require some clarification, particularly with regard to negative affect. Therefore, our studies include between-subjects manipulations, larger sample sizes, and measures of both positive and negative affect to better assess any possible distress that might accompany counterdispositional behavior."", ""location"": ""introduction"", ""reason"": ""The paper directly re-tests McNiel, Lowman, and Fleeson's (2010) empirical finding that manipulating state extraversion/introversion produces no state-by-trait interaction on negative affect (and increases positive affect regardless of disposition), explicitly critiquing the within-subject design and order effects in the prior negative-affect studies and conducting a stronger between-subjects test across Studies 1 and 2 that confirms the absence of a counterdispositional negative-affect cost (successful replication).""}","{""authors"": ""McNiel, J. M., Lowman, J. C., & Fleeson, W."", ""year"": ""2010"", ""title"": ""The effect of state extraversion on four types of affect."", ""doi"": ""10.1002/per.738"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Although possible, two studies have assessed negative affect under similar circumstances and have failed to find the state by trait interaction that would suggest a mood cost for acting counterdispositionally (McNiel & Fleeson, 2006; McNiel et al., 2010). Therefore, our studies include between-subjects manipulations, larger sample sizes, and measures of both positive and negative affect to better assess any possible distress that might accompany counterdispositional behavior."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""This study tested whether the pattern of relationships among extraversion, mood, behavior, and cognition found in Study 1 would generalize to a new interview situation. Very similar to previous research, participants reported more positive affect when acting extraverted, and this did not depend on their dispositional levels of extraversion. We again tested whether acting counterdispositionally might cause negative affect (along with positive affect in the case of introverts) and again failed to find any evidence for this idea."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The reported positive-affect benefit and/or absence of dispositional negative-affect cost agrees with this original’s findings. Cognitive depletion is a novel extension, not a failed replication of these mood findings.""}"
10.1038/sj.gene.6364398,c547481cb569f28e,1,,Functional annotation of a novel NFKB1 promoter polymorphism that increases risk for ulcerative colitis.,"Karban AS, Okazaki T, Panhuysen CI, Gallegos T, Potter JJ, Bailey-Wilson JE et al.",2004,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": """", ""title"": ""Functional annotation of a novel NFKB1 promoter polymorphism that increases risk for ulcerative colitis."", ""authors"": ""Karban AS, Okazaki T, Panhuysen CI, Gallegos T, Potter JJ, Bailey-Wilson JE et al."", ""year"": ""2004"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""failed""}, ""quote"": ""In terms of the putative NFKB1 94delATTG risk allele, Karban et al.25 first reported an association with risk of developing UC. We therefore tested this specific hypothesis in our entire set of samples but did not find significant evidence of association (OR = 0.99, 95% CI: 0.81-1.2; Supplementary Table 2)."", ""location"": ""results"", ""reason"": ""This is an omitted target: the paper explicitly re-tests the ulcerative colitis association of NFKB1 -94delATTG reported by Karban et al. (2004) in its new sample cohorts and finds no significant evidence of association.""}","{""authors"": ""Karban AS, Okazaki T, Panhuysen CI, Gallegos T, Potter JJ, Bailey-Wilson JE et al."", ""year"": ""2004"", ""title"": ""Functional annotation of a novel NFKB1 promoter polymorphism that increases risk for ulcerative colitis."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We first set out to confirm the published associations of , susceptibility to CD or UC with NFKB1 and TLR4 alleles. Specifically, we attempted to replicate the putative association of the -94delATTG allele of NFKB1 (NFKB1 94delATTG) with UC and the 299Gly allele of TLR4 (TLR4299G1y) with CD and UC.24,25"", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In terms of the putative NFKB1 94delATTG risk allele, Karban et al.25 first reported an association with risk of developing UC. We therefore tested this specific hypothesis in our entire set of samples but did not find significant evidence of association (OR = 0.99, 95% CI: 0.81-1.2; Supplementary Table 2)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The paper’s own samples did not support the original UC association; the pooled literature finding is not the outcome of the new-sample replication.""}"
10.1016/j.jml.2022.104352,23e78360aaf0d0af,1,10.1121/1.408177,Training Japanese listeners to identify English /r/ and /l/. II: The role of phonetic environment and talker variability in learning new perceptual categories,"Lively, S. E., Logan, J. S., & Pisoni, D. B.",1993,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": ""10.1121/1.408177"", ""title"": ""Training Japanese listeners to identify English /r/ and /l/. II: The role of phonetic environment and talker variability in learning new perceptual categories"", ""authors"": ""Lively, S. E., Logan, J. S., & Pisoni, D. B."", ""year"": ""1993"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""failed""}, ""quote"": ""The goal of the current paper is to replicate the key experiments from Logan et al. (1991) and Lively et al. (1993) and test the hypothesis that using high variability input (specifically, input with multiple talkers) leads to more generalisation in phonetic learning than when low variability input is used."", ""location"": ""Introduction (line 6)"", ""reason"": ""The paper explicitly re-tests both Logan et al. (1991) and Lively et al. (1993) (specifically Experiment 2 of Lively et al., 1993, providing the single-talker low-variability comparison and the familiar-versus-novel post-test contrast). As settled for the paired Logan et al. (1991) row, the replication outcome for Lively et al. (1993) is 'failed' rather than 'uninformative': the study was high-powered (N = 166, 99% power for the original effect size), found no evidence of the claimed multi-talker generalization advantage across both primary measures, and concluded that any true effect is much smaller than reported in the seminal studies, with the ambiguous Bayes factors reflecting cautious Bayesian interpretation rather than a methodological failure preventing adjudication.""}","{""authors"": ""Lively, S. E., Logan, J. S., & Pisoni, D. B."", ""year"": ""1993"", ""title"": ""Training Japanese listeners to identify English /r/ and /l/. II: The role of phonetic environment and talker variability in learning new perceptual categories"", ""doi"": ""10.1121/1.408177"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""2"", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The goal of the current paper is to replicate the key experiments from Logan et al. (1991) and Lively et al. (1993) and test the hypothesis that using high variability input (specifically, input with multiple talkers) leads to more generalisation in phonetic learning than when low variability input is used."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The current study attempted to replicate the findings of seminal work by Logan and colleagues (1991) and Lively and colleagues (1993), which found that phonetic training with multiple talkers led to greater generalization to novel talkers than single talker training. Across two different measures, our study found no evidence of such effect in a sample of 166 participants. Bayes Factor analyses found that in each case the evidence for a variability benefit was ambiguous."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Central multiple-talker advantage was not supported; ambiguous Bayes factors do not establish the null. Positive general phonetic learning is ancillary to the central contrast.""}"
10.1027/1618-3169/a000554,bb552c10b784b963,0,10.1016/s0022-5371(80)90403-x,Auditory encoding in visual short-term recall: Effects of noise intensity and spatial location.,"Colle, H. A.",1980,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 0, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": ""10.1016/s0022-5371(80)90403-x"", ""title"": ""Auditory encoding in visual short-term recall: Effects of noise intensity and spatial location."", ""authors"": ""Colle, H. A."", ""year"": ""1980"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined""}, ""quote"": ""In all seven experiments conducted so far, the changing-state effect on serial recall was independent of intensity (Colle, 1980; Ellermeier & Hellbrück, 1998; Schlittmeier et al., 2008; Tremblay & Jones, 1999), and this was also the case in the present study."", ""location"": ""discussion"", ""reason"": ""Omitted target: the paper frames its conceptual replication around the set of seven prior experiments across four papers (including Colle, 1980) and directly compares both its replicated changing-state result and its unexpected overall intensity effect against Colle (1980). Omitted-target identity retained; individual outcome held pending final source-scope review.""}","{""authors"": ""Colle, H. A."", ""year"": ""1980"", ""title"": ""Auditory encoding in visual short-term recall: Effects of noise intensity and spatial location."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/s0022-5371(80)90403-x"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""1, 2, 3"", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In all seven experiments conducted so far, the changing-state effect on serial recall was independent of intensity (Colle, 1980; Ellermeier & Hellbrück, 1998; Schlittmeier et al., 2008; Tremblay & Jones, 1999), and this was also the case in the present study."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We replicated the classic finding that the increased disruption by changing-state relative to steady-state sequences is independent of intensity. Contrary to previous studies, we found an unexpected main effect of intensity. Steady-state and changing-state sequences presented at 75 dB(A) were more disruptive than presented at 45 dB(A), suggesting that intensity plays a more important role than previously assumed in the disruption of working memory performance."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly confirm intensity-independent changing-state disruption but contrast their overall intensity effect with this original’s reported null intensity effect. Both comparisons concern the same target."", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """"}"
