": ""The target-specific overall verdict is explicitly printed in Appendix 1; effect-size criteria do not override this verdict.""}"
10.1007/s13164-018-0400-9,f0d7e94a060e237c,13,,Moral responsibility and determinism: The cognitive science of folk intuitions,"Nichols, S., & Knobe, J.",2007,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 13, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""type"": ""replication"", ""authors"": ""Nichols, S., & Knobe, J."", ""year"": ""2007"", ""title"": ""Moral responsibility and determinism: The cognitive science of folk intuitions"", ""doi"": """", ""outcome"": ""successful""}, ""quote"": ""Nichols, S., & Knobe, J. (2007). Moral responsibility and determinism: The cognitive science of folk intuitions. Nous, 41(4), 663-685. [Study 1] (Content-based, successful, osf.io/stjwg/)"", ""location"": ""Appendix 1 (line 145)"", ""reason"": ""Nichols and Knobe (2007) Study 1 is one of the 40 completed replications reported in Appendix 1 (with outcome 'successful') and was omitted from the shipped rows.""}","{""authors"": ""Nichols, S., & Knobe, J."", ""year"": ""2007"", ""title"": ""Moral responsibility and determinism: The cognitive science of folk intuitions"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""Study 1"", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Nichols, S., & Knobe, J. (2007). Moral responsibility and determinism: The cognitive science of folk intuitions. Nous, 41(4), 663-685. [Study 1] (Content-based, successful, osf.io/stjwg/)"", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Nichols, S., & Knobe, J. (2007). Moral responsibility and determinism: The cognitive science of folk intuitions. Nous, 41(4), 663-685. [Study 1] (Content-based, successful, osf.io/stjwg/)"", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""fulltext"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The target-specific overall verdict is explicitly printed in Appendix 1; effect-size criteria do not override this verdict.""}"
10.1027/1864-9335/a000178,9fae3aaf30064d5a,0,,Instructional manipulation checks: Detecting satisficing to increase statistical power.,"Oppenheimer, D. M., Meyvis, T., & Davidenko, N.",2009,full_text,same_author_year_siblings,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 0, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""type"": ""replication"", ""doi"": """", ""title"": ""Instructional manipulation checks: Detecting satisficing to increase statistical power."", ""authors"": ""Oppenheimer, D. M., Meyvis, T., & Davidenko, N."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""outcome"": ""successful""}, ""quote"": ""Oppenheimer et al. (2009; adapted from Thaler, 1985) asked participants to imagine that they have tickets to see their favorite football team play an important game, but that it is freezing cold on the day of the game. Participants rated their likelihood of attending the game on a 9-point scale (1 = definitely stay at home, 9 = definitely go to the game). Participants were marginally more likely to go to the game if they had paid for the ticket than if the ticket had been free."", ""location"": ""methods, lines 319-329"", ""reason"": ""The paper directly re-tests the sunk cost scenario from Oppenheimer, Meyvis, and Davidenko (2009) as Effect 1 of its thirteen replicated effects and reports a successful replication (t = 10.83, p < .001), making this an omitted target.""}","{""authors"": ""Oppenheimer, D. M., Meyvis, T., & Davidenko, N."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""Instructional manipulation checks: Detecting satisficing to increase statistical power."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Sunk costs (Oppenheimer, Meyvis, & Davidenko, 2009). Sunk costs are those that have already been incurred and cannot be recovered (Knox & Inkster, 1968). Oppenheimer et al. (2009; adapted from Thaler, 1985) asked participants to imagine that they have tickets to see their favorite football team play an important game, but that it is freezing cold on the day of the game. Participants rated their likelihood of attending the game on a 9-point scale (1 = definitely stay at home, 9 = definitely go to the game). Participants were marginally more likely to go to the game if they had paid for the ticket than if the ticket had been free."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""A large scale replication with 36 samples successfully replicated eleven of thirteen classic and contemporary effects in psychological science, some of which are well-known to be robust, and others that have been replicated infrequently or not at all. The original studies produced underestimates of some effects (e.g., anchoring-and-adjustment and allowed versus forbidden message framing), and overestimates of other effects (e.g., imagined contact producing willingness to interact with outgroups in the future). Two effects – flag priming influencing conservatism and currency priming influencing system justification – did not replicate."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The discussion gives an overall verdict of eleven successful effects and identifies the remaining two failures. This covers the named set. ""}"
10.1027/1864-9335/a000178,9fae3aaf30064d5a,5,,The current status of American public opinion.,"Hyman, H. H., & Sheatsley, P. B.",1950,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 5, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""type"": ""replication"", ""doi"": """", ""title"": ""The current status of American public opinion."", ""authors"": ""Hyman, H. H., & Sheatsley, P. B."", ""year"": ""1950"", ""outcome"": ""successful""}, ""quote"": ""In the original study, American participants answered two questions: whether communist countries should allow American reporters in and allow them to report the news back to American papers and whether America should allow communist reporters into the United States and allow them to report back to their papers. Participants reported more support for allowing communist reporters into America when that question was asked after the question about allowing American reporters into the communist countries."", ""location"": ""methods, lines 427-437"", ""reason"": ""The paper re-tests the norm of reciprocity effect from Hyman and Sheatsley (1950) as Effect 6 of the thirteen effects and successfully replicates it (χ2 = 135.3, p < .001), making this an omitted target.""}","{""authors"": ""Hyman, H. H., & Sheatsley, P. B."", ""year"": ""1950"", ""title"": ""The current status of American public opinion."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Norm of reciprocity (Hyman & Sheatsley, 1950). When confronted with a decision about allowing or denying the same behavior to an ingroup and outgroup, people may feel an obligation to reciprocity, or consistency in their evaluation of the behaviors (Hyman & Sheatsley, 1950). In the original study, American participants answered two questions: whether communist countries should allow American reporters in and allow them to report the news back to American papers and whether America should allow communist reporters into the United States and allow them to report back to their papers. Participants reported more support for allowing communist reporters into America when that question was asked after the question about allowing American reporters into the communist countries. In the replication, we changed the question slightly to ensure the “other country” was a suitable, modern target (North Korea). For international replication, the target country was determined by the researcher heading that replication to ensure suitability (see supplementary materials)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""A large scale replication with 36 samples successfully replicated eleven of thirteen classic and contemporary effects in psychological science, some of which are well-known to be robust, and others that have been replicated infrequently or not at all. The original studies produced underestimates of some effects (e.g., anchoring-and-adjustment and allowed versus forbidden message framing), and overestimates of other effects (e.g., imagined contact producing willingness to interact with outgroups in the future). Two effects – flag priming influencing conservatism and currency priming influencing system justification – did not replicate."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The discussion gives an overall verdict of eleven successful effects and identifies the remaining two failures. This covers the named set. ""}"
10.1027/1864-9335/a000178,9fae3aaf30064d5a,6,,Experiments in wording questions: II.,"Rugg, D.",1941,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 6, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""type"": ""replication"", ""doi"": """", ""title"": ""Experiments in wording questions: II."", ""authors"": ""Rugg, D."", ""year"": ""1941"", ""outcome"": ""successful""}, ""quote"": ""Rugg (1941) found that respondents were less likely to endorse forbidding speeches against democracy than they were to not endorse allowing speeches against democracy. Respondents in the United States were asked, in one condition, if the U.S. should allow speeches against democracy or, in another condition, whether the U.S. should forbid speeches against democracy. 62 percent of participants indicated “No” when asked if speeches against democracy should be allowed, but only 46 percent indicated “Yes” when asked if these speeches should be forbidden. In the replication, the words “The United States” were replaced with the name of the country the study was administered in."", ""location"": ""methods, lines 450-466"", ""reason"": ""The paper re-tests Rugg (1941) on allowed versus forbidden question wording as Effect 7 of the thirteen effects and explicitly notes its successful replication (χ2 = 3088.7, p < .001), making this an omitted target.""}","{""authors"": ""Rugg, D."", ""year"": ""1941"", ""title"": ""Experiments in wording questions: II."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Allowed/Forbidden (Rugg, 1941). Question phrasing can influence responses. Rugg (1941) found that respondents were less likely to endorse forbidding speeches against democracy than they were to not endorse allowing speeches against democracy. Respondents in the United States were asked, in one condition, if the U.S. should allow speeches against democracy or, in another condition, whether the U.S. should forbid speeches against democracy. 62 percent of participants indicated “No” when asked if speeches against democracy should be allowed, but only 46 percent indicated “Yes” when asked if these speeches should be forbidden. In the replication, the words “The United States” were replaced with the name of the country the study was administered in."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""A large scale replication with 36 samples successfully replicated eleven of thirteen classic and contemporary effects in psychological science, some of which are well-known to be robust, and others that have been replicated infrequently or not at all. The original studies produced underestimates of some effects (e.g., anchoring-and-adjustment and allowed versus forbidden message framing), and overestimates of other effects (e.g., imagined contact producing willingness to interact with outgroups in the future). Two effects – flag priming influencing conservatism and currency priming influencing system justification – did not replicate."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The discussion gives an overall verdict of eleven successful effects and identifies the remaining two failures. This covers the named set. ""}"
10.1027/1864-9335/a000178,9fae3aaf30064d5a,7,,"Prestige, suggestion, and attitudes.","Lorge, I., & Curtis, C. C.",1936,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 7, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""type"": ""replication"", ""doi"": """", ""title"": ""Prestige, suggestion, and attitudes."", ""authors"": ""Lorge, I., & Curtis, C. C."", ""year"": ""1936"", ""outcome"": ""successful""}, ""quote"": ""Lorge and Curtis (1936) examined how an identical quote would be perceived if it was attributed to a liked or disliked individual. Participants were asked to rate their agreement with a list of quotations. The quotation of interest was, “I hold it that a little rebellion, now and then, is a good thing, and as necessary in the political world as storms are in the physical world.” In one condition the quote was attributed to Thomas Jefferson, a liked individual, and in the other it was attributed to Vladimir Lenin, a disliked individual. More agreement was observed when the quote was attributed to Jefferson than Lenin (reported in Moskowitz, 2004)."", ""location"": ""methods, lines 470-484"", ""reason"": ""The paper re-tests the quote attribution effect of Lorge and Curtis (1936) as Effect 8 of the thirteen effects and successfully replicates it (t = 12.79, p < .001), making this an omitted target.""}","{""authors"": ""Lorge, I., & Curtis, C. C."", ""year"": ""1936"", ""title"": ""Prestige, suggestion, and attitudes."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Quote Attribution (Lorge & Curtis, 1936). The source of information has a great impact on how that information is perceived and evaluated. Lorge and Curtis (1936) examined how an identical quote would be perceived if it was attributed to a liked or disliked individual. Participants were asked to rate their agreement with a list of quotations. The quotation of interest was, “I hold it that a little rebellion, now and then, is a good thing, and as necessary in the political world as storms are in the physical world.” In one condition the quote was attributed to Thomas Jefferson, a liked individual, and in the other it was attributed to Vladimir Lenin, a disliked individual. More agreement was observed when the quote was attributed to Jefferson than Lenin (reported in Moskowitz, 2004). In the replication, we used a quote attributed to either George Washington (liked individual) or Osama Bin Laden (disliked individual)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""A large scale replication with 36 samples successfully replicated eleven of thirteen classic and contemporary effects in psychological science, some of which are well-known to be robust, and others that have been replicated infrequently or not at all. The original studies produced underestimates of some effects (e.g., anchoring-and-adjustment and allowed versus forbidden message framing), and overestimates of other effects (e.g., imagined contact producing willingness to interact with outgroups in the future). Two effects – flag priming influencing conservatism and currency priming influencing system justification – did not replicate."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The discussion gives an overall verdict of eleven successful effects and identifies the remaining two failures. This covers the named set. ""}"
10.1016/j.joep.2018.11.004,f7694ca5389b4aa3,1,,Digital Discrimination: The Case of Airbnb.com.,"Edelman, B., & Luca, M.",2014,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": """", ""title"": ""Digital Discrimination: The Case of Airbnb.com."", ""authors"": ""Edelman, B., & Luca, M."", ""year"": ""2014"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""successful""}, ""quote"": ""However, we did replicate previous findings showing that Black hosts charge lower prices for their apartments (Edelman & Luca, 2014)."", ""location"": ""Conclusion, p. 20, lines 623-624"", ""reason"": ""Omitted target: the paper explicitly states that its test of host race on apartment prices replicates Edelman and Luca (2014) and concludes that it successfully replicated their finding that Black hosts charge lower prices.""}","{""authors"": ""Edelman, B., & Luca, M."", ""year"": ""2014"", ""title"": ""Digital Discrimination: The Case of Airbnb.com."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Finally, we test for an effect of host race on apartment prices. This provides a replication of previous studies reporting that, compared to White host, Black and Asian hosts charge lower prices for similar apartments (Edelman & Luca, 2014; Kakar, Franco, Voelz, & Wu, 2016; Wang, Xi, & Gilheany, 2015)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""However, we did replicate previous findings showing that Black hosts charge lower prices for their apartments (Edelman & Luca, 2014)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Reference prints a malformed DOI prefix attached to an SSRN URL, not a DOI; omitted.""}"
10.1016/j.joep.2018.11.004,f7694ca5389b4aa3,2,,The visible host: Does race guide Airbnb rental rates in San Francisco?,"Kakar, V., Franco, J., Voelz, J., & Wu, J.",2016,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 2, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": """", ""title"": ""The visible host: Does race guide Airbnb rental rates in San Francisco?"", ""authors"": ""Kakar, V., Franco, J., Voelz, J., & Wu, J."", ""year"": ""2016"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""mixed""}, ""quote"": ""Finally, we test for an effect of host race on apartment prices. This provides a replication of previous studies reporting that, compared to White host, Black and Asian hosts charge lower prices for similar apartments (Edelman & Luca, 2014; Kakar, Franco, Voelz, & Wu, 2016; Wang, Xi, & Gilheany, 2015)."", ""location"": ""The current study, p. 6, lines 178-181"", ""reason"": ""Omitted target: the paper explicitly frames its host-race price analysis as a replication of Kakar et al. (2016) regarding lower prices for minority hosts and directly compares its findings against Kakar et al. (2016) in the discussion.""}","{""authors"": ""Kakar, V., Franco, J., Voelz, J., & Wu, J."", ""year"": ""2016"", ""title"": ""The visible host: Does race guide Airbnb rental rates in San Francisco?"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Finally, we test for an effect of host race on apartment prices. This provides a replication of previous studies reporting that, compared to White host, Black and Asian hosts charge lower prices for similar apartments (Edelman & Luca, 2014; Kakar, Franco, Voelz, & Wu, 2016; Wang, Xi, & Gilheany, 2015)."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Examining the influence of these other cues, we found that Black hosts charge on average 10.09% less for their apartments. This in line with the price gap of approximately 12% reported FACIAL ATTRACTIVENESS & TRUSTWORTHINESS ON AIRBNB 17 by Edelman and Luca (2014). However, in contrast to previous studies that analyzed Airbnb listing in San Francisco, Oakland, and Berkeley (Kakar et al., 2016; Wang et al., 2015), we did not find that Asian hosts charge less for their apartments."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The introduction places this paper in the set reporting lower Black and Asian host prices; Black penalty agrees while Asian penalty does not.""}"
10.1016/j.joep.2018.11.004,f7694ca5389b4aa3,3,,The model minority? Not on Airbnb.com: A hedonic pricing model to quantify racial bias against Asian Americans.,"Wang, D., Xi, S., & Gilheany, J.",2015,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 3, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": """", ""title"": ""The model minority? Not on Airbnb.com: A hedonic pricing model to quantify racial bias against Asian Americans."", ""authors"": ""Wang, D., Xi, S., & Gilheany, J."", ""year"": ""2015"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""failed""}, ""quote"": ""However, in contrast to previous studies that analyzed Airbnb listing in San Francisco, Oakland, and Berkeley (Kakar et al., 2016; Wang et al., 2015), we did not find that Asian hosts charge less for their apartments."", ""location"": ""General Discussion, p. 17, lines 508-510"", ""reason"": ""Omitted target: the paper explicitly states that testing for an effect of host race on apartment prices replicates Wang et al. (2015) and reports that, in contrast to Wang et al. (2015), Asian hosts did not charge lower prices.""}","{""authors"": ""Wang, D., Xi, S., & Gilheany, J."", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": ""The model minority? Not on Airbnb.com: A hedonic pricing model to quantify racial bias against Asian Americans."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Finally, we test for an effect of host race on apartment prices. This provides a replication of previous studies reporting that, compared to White host, Black and Asian hosts charge lower prices for similar apartments (Edelman & Luca, 2014; Kakar, Franco, Voelz, & Wu, 2016; Wang, Xi, & Gilheany, 2015)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""However, in contrast to previous studies that analyzed Airbnb listing in San Francisco, Oakland, and Berkeley (Kakar et al., 2016; Wang et al., 2015), we did not find that Asian hosts charge less for their apartments."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The specific Asian-host price penalty is explicitly contrasted with this target.""}"
10.1080/23743603.2021.1878340,662ac9ba6f649399,0,10.1006/obhd.1995.1075,Seeking subjective dominance in multidimensional space: An explanation of the asymmetric dominance effect.,"Ariely, D., & Wallsten, T. S.",1995,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 0, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": ""10.1006/obhd.1995.1075"", ""title"": ""Seeking subjective dominance in multidimensional space: An explanation of the asymmetric dominance effect."", ""authors"": ""Ariely, D., & Wallsten, T. S."", ""year"": ""1995""}, ""quote"": ""This article reports two very close replication studies of the decoy effect, targeting\n\nExperiment 1 in Ariely and Wallsten (1995) and Experiment 1 in Connolly et al. (2013)."", ""location"": ""Introduction, p. 5 (lines 173-175)"", ""reason"": ""In addition to replicating Connolly et al. (2013) in Study 2, the paper conducts a full direct replication of Experiment 1 from Ariely and Wallsten (1995) in Study 1 (reporting a failed replication outcome), making it an omitted replication target.""}","{""authors"": ""Ariely, D., & Wallsten, T. S."", ""year"": ""1995"", ""title"": ""Seeking subjective dominance in multidimensional space: An explanation of the asymmetric dominance effect."", ""doi"": ""10.1006/obhd.1995.1075"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""Experiment 1"", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1"", ""evidence_quote"": ""This article reports two very close replication studies of the decoy effect, targeting\n\nExperiment 1 in Ariely and Wallsten (1995) and Experiment 1 in Connolly et al. (2013)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We conducted a very close direct replication of Experiment 1 in Ariely and Wallsten\n\n(1995). We failed to find support for the original results."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly give an overall verdict for this specific replication.""}"
10.1002/rrq.62,03263a7c5c7d8646,0,10.3102/01623737028004335,Effects of a voluntary summer reading intervention on reading achievement: Results from a randomized field trial.,"Kim, J. S.",2006,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 0, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": ""10.3102/01623737028004335"", ""title"": ""Effects of a voluntary summer reading intervention on reading achievement: Results from a randomized field trial."", ""authors"": ""Kim, J. S."", ""year"": ""2006"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""failed""}, ""quote"": ""We also compared the performance of control group students in moderate and high poverty schools in this study with the two studies of READS that we were attempting to replicate. As shown in Figure 5, in the initial studies, as in high poverty schools in this study, control group students lost ground in the summer, d = - .07 (Kim, 2006) and d = - .08 (Kim & White, 2008)."", ""location"": ""Results > Differences in Control Group Performance, p. 34, lines 806-809"", ""reason"": ""The paper explicitly states that the randomized trial was designed to replicate two initial experimental studies of READS—Kim (2006) and Kim and White (2008)—making Kim (2006) an omitted replication target with a failed outcome.""}","{""authors"": ""Kim, J. S."", ""year"": ""2006"", ""title"": ""Effects of a voluntary summer reading intervention on reading achievement: Results from a randomized field trial."", ""doi"": ""10.3102/01623737028004335"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We also compared the performance of control group students in moderate and high poverty\nschools in this study with the two studies of READS that we were attempting to replicate. As\nshown in Figure 5, in the initial studies, as in high poverty schools in this study, control group\nstudents lost ground in the summer, d = - .07 (Kim, 2006) and d = - .08 (Kim & White, 2008)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In the\nCoyne et al. (2013) study, as in our study, differences in the response of comparison group\nstudents explained the failure to replicate."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors give an overall failure-to-replicate verdict for their study in the conclusions. Positive effects in high-poverty schools are discussed, but the central effect did not replicate in the full sample; the overall verdict governs.""}"
10.1016/j.cogdev.2017.10.004,63ba7fc3511ae27e,0,,Eighteen-month-old infants show false belief understanding in an active helping paradigm.,"Buttelmann, D., Carpenter, M., & Tomasello, M.",2009,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 0, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Buttelmann, D., Carpenter, M., & Tomasello, M."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""Eighteen-month-old infants show false belief understanding in an active helping paradigm."", ""doi"": """", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""successful""}, ""quote"": ""We also replicated Buttelmann and colleagues' (2009) finding that 18-month-old infants helped an experimenter with a false belief by unlocking the actual location of her desired object, not the empty box she misguidedly tried to open."", ""location"": ""Section 4 General discussion, p. 8 (chunk 27, view line 30)"", ""reason"": ""Omitted target: Experiment 1 (Task 1) is an explicit close replication of the false-belief helping condition of Buttelmann et al. (2009) and reports replicating the original behavioral finding.""}","{""authors"": ""Buttelmann, D., Carpenter, M., & Tomasello, M."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""Eighteen-month-old infants show false belief understanding in an active helping paradigm."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Our interactive task was based closely on a study by Buttelmann et al. (2009)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We also replicated Buttelmann and colleagues' (2009) finding that 18-month-old infants helped an experimenter with a false belief by unlocking the actual location of her desired object, not the empty box she misguidedly tried to open."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Author verdict explicitly identifies this original; limitations do not replace that verdict.""}"
10.1016/j.cogdev.2017.10.004,63ba7fc3511ae27e,2,,Do 15-month-old infants understand false beliefs?,"Onishi, K. H., & Baillargeon, R.",2005,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 2, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Onishi, K. H., & Baillargeon, R."", ""year"": ""2005"", ""title"": ""Do 15-month-old infants understand false beliefs?"", ""doi"": """", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""failed""}, ""quote"": ""On the other hand, we failed to reproduce evidence that 18-month-old infants’ expectations are violated when an experimenter who ought to have a false belief reaches for an object in its true location (e.g. Onishi & Baillargeon, 2005; Song et al., 2008; Träuble et al., 2010)."", ""location"": ""Section 4 General discussion, p. 8 (chunk 27, view line 30)"", ""reason"": ""Omitted target: Experiment 1 (Tasks 2 and 3) directly adapts and re-tests the violation-of-expectation false-belief paradigm of Onishi and Baillargeon (2005) and explicitly reports failing to replicate the effect.""}","{""authors"": ""Onishi, K. H., & Baillargeon, R."", ""year"": ""2005"", ""title"": ""Do 15-month-old infants understand false beliefs?"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1"", ""evidence_quote"": ""We conducted close replications of studies by Knudsen and Liszkowski (2012) and Buttelmann et al. (2009), as well as two experiments based on published violation of expectation paradigms (Onishi & Baillargeon, 2005; Song, Onishi, Baillargeon & Fisher, 2008; Träuble, Marinović & Pauen, 2010), with procedural changes based on both our novel experimental goals and the original authors’ advice."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""On the other hand, we failed to reproduce evidence that 18-month-old infants’ expectations are violated when an experimenter who ought to have a false belief reaches for an object in its true location (e.g. Onishi & Baillargeon, 2005; Song et al., 2008; Träuble et al., 2010)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Author verdict explicitly identifies this original; limitations do not replace that verdict.""}"
10.1016/j.cogdev.2017.10.004,63ba7fc3511ae27e,3,,Can an agent’s false belief be corrected by an appropriate communication? Psychological reasoning in 18-month-old infants.,"Song, H. J., Onishi, K. H., Baillargeon, R., & Fisher, C.",2008,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 3, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Song, H. J., Onishi, K. H., Baillargeon, R., & Fisher, C."", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": ""Can an agent’s false belief be corrected by an appropriate communication? Psychological reasoning in 18-month-old infants."", ""doi"": """", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""failed""}, ""quote"": ""On the other hand, we failed to reproduce evidence that 18-month-old infants’ expectations are violated when an experimenter who ought to have a false belief reaches for an object in its true location (e.g. Onishi & Baillargeon, 2005; Song et al., 2008; Träuble et al., 2010)."", ""location"": ""Section 4 General discussion, p. 8 (chunk 27, view line 30)"", ""reason"": ""Omitted target: The authors explicitly identify Song et al. (2008) as one of the published violation-of-expectation studies upon which their Experiment 1 VOE tasks (specifically Task 3's transfer procedure) were based, and report failing to reproduce its false-belief expectation finding.""}","{""authors"": ""Song, H. J., Onishi, K. H., Baillargeon, R., & Fisher, C."", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": ""Can an agent’s false belief be corrected by an appropriate communication? Psychological reasoning in 18-month-old infants."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1"", ""evidence_quote"": ""We conducted close replications of studies by Knudsen and Liszkowski (2012) and Buttelmann et al. (2009), as well as two experiments based on published violation of expectation paradigms (Onishi & Baillargeon, 2005; Song, Onishi, Baillargeon & Fisher, 2008; Träuble, Marinović & Pauen, 2010), with procedural changes based on both our novel experimental goals and the original authors’ advice."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""On the other hand, we failed to reproduce evidence that 18-month-old infants’ expectations are violated when an experimenter who ought to have a false belief reaches for an object in its true location (e.g. Onishi & Baillargeon, 2005; Song et al., 2008; Träuble et al., 2010)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Author verdict explicitly identifies this original; limitations do not replace that verdict.""}"
10.1016/j.cogdev.2017.10.004,63ba7fc3511ae27e,4,,Early theory of mind competencies: do infants understand others’ beliefs?,"Träuble, B., Marinović, V., & Pauen, S.",2010,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 4, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Träuble, B., Marinović, V., & Pauen, S."", ""year"": ""2010"", ""title"": ""Early theory of mind competencies: do infants understand others’ beliefs?"", ""doi"": """", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""failed""}, ""quote"": ""On the other hand, we failed to reproduce evidence that 18-month-old infants’ expectations are violated when an experimenter who ought to have a false belief reaches for an object in its true location (e.g. Onishi & Baillargeon, 2005; Song et al., 2008; Träuble et al., 2010)."", ""location"": ""Section 4 General discussion, p. 8 (chunk 27, view line 30)"", ""reason"": ""Omitted target: The authors explicitly identify Träuble et al. (2010) as one of the violation-of-expectation studies re-tested in Experiment 1 (and in a preceding direct replication pilot) and report failing to reproduce its finding.""}","{""authors"": ""Träuble, B., Marinović, V., & Pauen, S."", ""year"": ""2010"", ""title"": ""Early theory of mind competencies: do infants understand others’ beliefs?"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1"", ""evidence_quote"": ""We conducted close replications of studies by Knudsen and Liszkowski (2012) and Buttelmann et al. (2009), as well as two experiments based on published violation of expectation paradigms (Onishi & Baillargeon, 2005; Song, Onishi, Baillargeon & Fisher, 2008; Träuble, Marinović & Pauen, 2010), with procedural changes based on both our novel experimental goals and the original authors’ advice."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""On the other hand, we failed to reproduce evidence that 18-month-old infants’ expectations are violated when an experimenter who ought to have a false belief reaches for an object in its true location (e.g. Onishi & Baillargeon, 2005; Song et al., 2008; Träuble et al., 2010)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Author verdict explicitly identifies this original; limitations do not replace that verdict.""}"
10.18718/81781.38,67732009dff134e5,1,10.1002/bdm.495,When Efortful Thinking Influences Judgmental Anchoring: Differential Effects of Forewarning and Incentives on Self-generated and Externally Provided Anchors,"Epley, N. & Gilovich, T.",2005,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": ""10.1002/bdm.495"", ""title"": ""When Efortful Thinking Influences Judgmental Anchoring: Differential Effects of Forewarning and Incentives on Self-generated and Externally Provided Anchors"", ""authors"": ""Epley, N. & Gilovich, T."", ""year"": ""2005"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""failed""}, ""quote"": ""Study 3 is a replication of Epley and Gilovich (2005, Study 2) and investigated the moderator forewarning."", ""location"": ""Section 3 Resolving the Contradictions, p. 4 (lines 166-167)"", ""reason"": ""In Study 3, the paper conducts a preregistered replication of a separate original article, Epley and Gilovich (2005, Study 2; DOI: 10.1002/bdm.495), which is an omitted target in the shipped dataset and resulted in a failed replication.""}","{""authors"": ""Epley, N. & Gilovich, T."", ""year"": ""2005"", ""title"": ""When Efortful Thinking Influences Judgmental Anchoring: Differential Effects of Forewarning and Incentives on Self-generated and Externally Provided Anchors"", ""doi"": ""10.1002/bdm.495"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""Study 2"", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""In their Study 2, Epley and Gilovich (2005) found that warning participants that their adjustments\nfrom anchors were insufficient led to increases in adjustments from self-generated anchors but not\nfrom experimenter-provided anchors. We conducted a preregistered (https://osf.io/f5sj8) replica-\ntion of this study."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Thus, we deem\nthe replication an informative failure: There is evidence against the hypothesis that forewarning\nmoderates adjustment from self-generated anchors."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The moderators forming the central finding did not replicate. General anchoring effects are checks of the paradigm, not a successful replication of the moderator claim.""}"
10.1080/15248372.2018.1544140,bac6178abc69a5fe,1,10.1111/j.2044-835X.2011.02046.x,Where will the triangle look for it? Attributing false beliefs to a geometric shape at 17 months,"Surian, L., & Geraci, A.",2012,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": ""10.1111/j.2044-835X.2011.02046.x"", ""title"": ""Where will the triangle look for it? Attributing false beliefs to a geometric shape at 17 months"", ""authors"": ""Surian, L., & Geraci, A."", ""year"": ""2012"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""failed""}, ""quote"": ""Crucially, however, even after adding verbal narration, original ﬁndings could still not reliably be replicated, particularly in the Surian and Geraci task—much in line with previous large-scale failed replication attempts of anticipatory looking false belief tasks (Kulke et al., 2018). A lack of looking patterns in line with false belief processing in the Surian and Geraci (2012) task may be because the eﬀect is fragile and hence diﬃcult to measure."", ""location"": ""Discussion, p. 11, lines 546-551"", ""reason"": ""The paper collects new eye-tracking data to re-test the anticipatory-looking false-belief task of Surian and Geraci (2012) with added verbal narration and concludes that its original findings could not be replicated, making it an omitted replication target with a failed outcome.""}","{""authors"": ""Surian, L., & Geraci, A."", ""year"": ""2012"", ""title"": ""Where will the triangle look for it? Attributing false beliefs to a geometric shape at 17 months"", ""doi"": ""10.1111/j.2044-835X.2011.02046.x"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The rationale of the present study was, therefore, to explore whether existing successful\nvs. unsuccessful replications of AL FB tasks may be due to diﬀerences in verbal narration.\nTo this end, we followed up on our previous large-scale replication attempts of the two AL\ntasks by Southgate et al. (2007) and by Surian and Geraci (2012) (Kulke, Reiß, Krist, &\nRakoczy, 2017; Kulke et al., 2018, 2018)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Crucially, however, even after adding verbal narration, original ﬁndings could still not\nreliably be replicated, particularly in the Surian and Geraci task—much in line with\nprevious large-scale failed replication attempts of anticipatory looking false belief tasks\n(Kulke et al., 2018)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Target-specific discussion verdict: only FB1 replicated for Southgate; original belief-based looking failed for Surian and Geraci.""}"
10.1371/journal.pone.0172636,1262c4f34ab4cc78,1,,"The retrospective gambler’s fallacy: Unlikely events, constructing the past, and multiple universes.","Oppenheimer DM, Monin B.",2009,full_text,,error,obvious,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""obvious"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Oppenheimer DM, Monin B."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""The retrospective gambler’s fallacy: Unlikely events, constructing the past, and multiple universes."", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined""}, ""quote"": ""To help indicate the overall quality of our replication we included as a positive control an additional experiment with a well-defined effect size. Specifically, we used the retrospective gambler’s task [ 11 ]."", ""location"": ""Methods: Positive control; Results Table3; reference11"", ""reason"": ""Parent independently inspected new random assignment to the casino-dice positive-control experiment and the authors explicit detection of its expected retrospective gamblers effect in Table3. Reference11 identifies Oppenheimer/Monin2009. The sole shipped pair row1965 concerns Gervais/Norenzayan2012 religious-disbelief image priming, a different author team, published finding and task. This is an empirically tested published positive-control finding, not method reuse or an alternate report. Identity-only addition; outcome remains ungraded in this review.""}","{""authors"": ""Oppenheimer DM, Monin B."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""The retrospective gambler’s fallacy: Unlikely events, constructing the past, and multiple universes."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""To help indicate the overall quality of our replication we included as a positive control an additional experiment with a well-defined effect size. Specifically, we used the retrospective gambler’s task [ 11 ]."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Our inability to detect a substantial decline in religious belief was not due to quality issues as we detected an expected effect in an additional positive control study with the same participants ( F (1,963) = 21.2, p < .001, Table 3 )."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.1353/eca.2016.0000,2050e50097e7846c,0,,How Elastic Are Preferences for Redistribution? Evidence from Randomized Survey Experiments.,"Kuziemko, Ilyana, Michael I. Norton, Emmanuel Saez, and Stefanie Stantcheva.",2013,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 0, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": """", ""title"": ""How Elastic Are Preferences for Redistribution? Evidence from Randomized Survey Experiments."", ""authors"": ""Kuziemko, Ilyana, Michael I. Norton, Emmanuel Saez, and Stefanie Stantcheva."", ""year"": ""2013"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""successful""}, ""quote"": ""In reality, demand for income redistribution in the United States has remained ﬂat by some measures and decreased according to others (see Kuziemko and others 2013), as we document later in this paper."", ""location"": ""Introduction, p. 368"", ""reason"": ""The paper explicitly cites Kuziemko et al. (2013) for the flat or decreasing U.S. trend in redistribution demand that it documents and replicates in Section I.A, making it a valid omitted target.""}","{""authors"": ""Kuziemko, Ilyana, Michael I. Norton, Emmanuel Saez, and Stefanie Stantcheva."", ""year"": ""2013"", ""title"": ""How Elastic Are Preferences for Redistribution? Evidence from Randomized Survey Experiments."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In reality, demand for\nincome redistribution in the United States has remained ﬂat by some mea-\nsures and decreased according to others (see Kuziemko and others 2013),\nas we document later in this paper.\n\nAcross the four measures, we are able\nto replicate the ﬁnding of previous literature showing no increase in sup-\nport for redistribution over this period of increased inequality."", ""outcome"": """", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": ""not checked"", ""outcome_computational_quote"": ""To ensure that our heterogeneous patterns\nare not driven by data or coding differences between our paper and previ-\nous work, we ﬁrst demonstrate that we can replicate the earlier ﬁnding of\nﬂat aggregate demand using our survey measures."", ""outcome_robustness"": ""robust"", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": ""Across the four measures, we are able\nto replicate the ﬁnding of previous literature showing no increase in sup-\nport for redistribution over this period of increased inequality."", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The earlier flat aggregate trend survives alternative measures and survey datasets. Exact original numerical estimates are not the object of the analysis.""}"
10.1080/14459795.2022.2135227,cd0616d25cb2d32e,1,10.1098/rsos.200090,A direct and conceptual replication of post-loss speeding when gambling.,"Eben, C., Chen, Z., Vermeylen, L., Billieux, J., & Verbruggen, F.",2020,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": ""10.1098/rsos.200090"", ""title"": ""A direct and conceptual replication of post-loss speeding when gambling."", ""authors"": ""Eben, C., Chen, Z., Vermeylen, L., Billieux, J., & Verbruggen, F."", ""year"": ""2020"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""successful""}, ""quote"": ""Across experi-\nments, we could again replicate the finding by Langer and Roth (1975) in an online ex-\nperimental setting (see also our replication study). Thus, introducing a start response\ndid not alter the illusion-of-control effect. Moreover, we replicated the finding that par-\nticipants speed up after losses compared to wins (Eben et al., 2020; Verbruggen et al.,\n2017)."", ""location"": ""Section 4. General Discussion, p. 16"", ""reason"": ""Both experiments explicitly re-test and successfully replicate the post-loss speeding effect using the start-response procedure and exclusion criteria of Eben et al. (2020). Under the replication-chain rule, Eben et al. (2020) is the nearest target in the chain re-testing Verbruggen et al. (2017) and is an omitted target in the shipped dataset.""}","{""authors"": ""Eben, C., Chen, Z., Vermeylen, L., Billieux, J., & Verbruggen, F."", ""year"": ""2020"", ""title"": ""A direct and conceptual replication of post-loss speeding when gambling."", ""doi"": ""10.1098/rsos.200090"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Across experi-\nments, we could again replicate the finding by Langer and Roth (1975) in an online ex-\nperimental setting (see also our replication study). Thus, introducing a start response\ndid not alter the illusion-of-control effect. Moreover, we replicated the finding that par-\nticipants speed up after losses compared to wins (Eben et al., 2020; Verbruggen et al.,\n2017)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Across experi-\nments, we could again replicate the finding by Langer and Roth (1975) in an online ex-\nperimental setting (see also our replication study). Thus, introducing a start response\ndid not alter the illusion-of-control effect. Moreover, we replicated the finding that par-\nticipants speed up after losses compared to wins (Eben et al., 2020; Verbruggen et al.,\n2017)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Across both experiments the authors explicitly replicate speeding after losses relative to wins.""}"
10.1086/695137,03148576e581e7dc,1,,Gender-Biased Breastfeeding in Egypt: Examining the Fertility Preference Hypotheses of Jayachandran and Kuziemko (2011).,"Chakravarty, Abhishek.",2015,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Chakravarty, Abhishek."", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": ""Gender-Biased Breastfeeding in Egypt: Examining the Fertility Preference Hypotheses of Jayachandran and Kuziemko (2011)."", ""doi"": """", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""mixed""}, ""quote"": ""However, Chakravarty’s speciﬁcation omits the gender main effect while including the interactions of gender with birth order. Adding the gender dummy in column 3, we essentially ﬁnd results consistent with column 1, that is, there is no evidence for a peak in male advantage in breastfeeding at middle birth order. Thus, this is the only prediction of JK that we fail to support in our analysis.\n\nG. Summary\nWe ﬁnd evidence supporting all predictions in JK (1, 2, 3, 5, 6, and 7) but 9, the additional prediction in Chakravarty (4), and our new prediction (8)."", ""location"": ""Section V.F-G, p. 206 (lines 1394-1403); see also Section V.C, p. 199 (lines 1043-1048), Section V.F, p. 205 (lines 1344-1352), and Section VII, p. 212 (lines 1751-1755)"", ""reason"": ""Chakravarty (2015) is a replicated target tested on two components in the Pakistan DHS sample: (1) Chakravarty's additional sibling-sex-composition hypothesis (prediction 4, eq. 3), which is confirmed in Table 5 col. 5 (Section V.C), Section V.G, and the Conclusion (Section VII), and (2) Chakravarty's quadratic birth-order interaction specification for prediction 9 (Section V.F, Table 9 cols. 2-3). In Table 9 col. 2, replicating Chakravarty's original specification reproduces a strongly statistically significant gender-by-birth-order interaction (p = .000 jointly) in line with a middle-birth-order peak, but the authors show that Chakravarty's specification omits the gender main effect and that adding the gender dummy in col. 3 eliminates the middle-birth-order peak. Under the codebook's main-message rule, 'statistically successful but flawed' applies only when the methodological critique is the paper's main message; here, the omitted-main-effect critique concerns only one regression specification in Section V.F and is not the main message of the paper or of the Chakravarty comparison (which highlights confirmation of prediction 4 in the Summary and Conclusion). Because prediction 4 is supported while the middle-birth-order peak is rejected once properly specified, the overall outcome for Chakravarty (2015) is 'mixed'.""}","{""authors"": ""Chakravarty, Abhishek."", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": ""Gender-Biased Breastfeeding in Egypt: Examining the Fertility Preference Hypotheses of Jayachandran and Kuziemko (2011)."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Further, according to the fourth prediction (Chakravarty 2015), we may\nexpect the effect of children’s sex composition on breastfeeding duration to\nbe different for boys and girls. The results in the last column of table 5 are in-\ndeed consistent with Chakravarty (2015); girls are breastfed for an additional\n0.85 months compared to boys as the ratio of elder male siblings increases from\n0 to 1, keeping all else ﬁxed."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""However, Chakravarty’s speciﬁcation omits the gender main effect while\nincluding the interactions of gender with birth order. Adding the gender\ndummy in column 3, we essentially ﬁnd results consistent with column 1, that\nis, there is no evidence for a peak in male advantage in breastfeeding at middle\nbirth order. Thus, this is the only prediction of JK that we fail to support in\nour analysis.\n\nG. Summary\nWe ﬁnd evidence supporting all predictions in JK (1, 2, 3, 5, 6, and 7) but 9,\nthe additional prediction in Chakravarty (4), and our new prediction (8)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Additional sex-composition prediction is supported; birth-order result is obtained with the original specification but rejected after adding the omitted gender main effect. Both findings are explicitly retested.""}"
10.1167/jov.20.6.18,84cdf9f12aedb720,0,10.1167/6.10.4,Kin recognition and the perceived facial similarity of children,"Maloney, L. T., & Dal Martello, M. F.",2006,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 0, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": ""10.1167/6.10.4"", ""title"": ""Kin recognition and the perceived facial similarity of children"", ""authors"": ""Maloney, L. T., & Dal Martello, M. F."", ""year"": ""2006"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""mixed""}, ""quote"": ""Facial similarity between individuals informs kinship judgments in third-party kin recognition. Indeed, one study found that similarity and kinship judgments encapsulate the same information (Maloney & Dal Martello, 2006). Yet, another study found that this is not the case when comparing adult face pairs of different sex (DeBruine et al., 2009). We replicated these studies to further clarify the role of facial similarity in kin recognition."", ""location"": ""abstract"", ""reason"": ""The paper explicitly states that it replicates Maloney and Dal Martello's (2006) child-face study alongside DeBruine et al. (2009), and compares its correlation and age-difference results directly against Maloney and Dal Martello (2006), making it an omitted replication target with a mixed outcome.""}","{""authors"": ""Maloney, L. T., & Dal Martello, M. F."", ""year"": ""2006"", ""title"": ""Kin recognition and the perceived facial similarity of children"", ""doi"": ""10.1167/6.10.4"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Facial similarity between individuals informs kinship judgments in third-party kin recognition. Indeed, one study found that similarity and kinship judgments encapsulate the same information (Maloney & Dal Martello, 2006). Yet, another study found that this is not the case when comparing adult face pairs of different sex (DeBruine et al., 2009). We replicated these studies to further clarify the role of facial similarity in kin recognition."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In conclusion, using a larger stimuli sample of children, we replicated Maloney and Dal Martello's (\n2006\n) finding that kinship and similarity ratings are highly correlated. We found that the effect of sex was the same on similarity and on kinship judgments, contrary to\nDeBruine et al (2009)\n. However, we found that the effect of age was different on similarity judgments than on kinship judgments, contrary to\nMaloney and Dal Martello (2006)\n."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": """"}"
10.1002/jaba.613,b99fe2c5d5675e4b,1,10.1002/jaba.67,Effects of two variations of differential reinforcement on prompt dependency,"Cividini-Motta, C., & Ahearn, W. H.",2013,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": ""10.1002/jaba.67"", ""title"": ""Effects of two variations of differential reinforcement on prompt dependency"", ""authors"": ""Cividini-Motta, C., & Ahearn, W. H."", ""year"": ""2013"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""descriptive only""}, ""quote"": ""The current research sought to systematically replicate Smaby et al. (2007) and Cividini-Motta and Ahearn’s (2013) use of the MSA, speci ﬁ cally to evaluate the relative reinforcing ef ﬁ cacy of the individual components of a token economy in a brief assessment."", ""location"": ""introduction"", ""reason"": ""While the abstract only cites Smaby et al. (2007), the full text explicitly pairs Cividini-Motta and Ahearn (2013) with Smaby et al. (2007) as the two studies whose use of the MSA was systematically replicated, making it an omitted replication target with a 'descriptive only' outcome.""}","{""authors"": ""Cividini-Motta, C., & Ahearn, W. H."", ""year"": ""2013"", ""title"": ""Effects of two variations of differential reinforcement on prompt dependency"", ""doi"": ""10.1002/jaba.67"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The current research sought to systematically replicate Smaby et al. (2007) and Cividini-Motta and Ahearn’s (2013) use of the MSA, speci ﬁ cally to evaluate the relative reinforcing ef ﬁ cacy of the individual components of a token economy in a brief assessment."", ""outcome"": ""descriptive only"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The current research sought to systematically replicate Smaby et al. (2007) and Cividini-Motta and Ahearn’s (2013) use of the MSA, speci ﬁ cally to evaluate the relative reinforcing ef ﬁ cacy of the individual components of a token economy in a brief assessment."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""introduction"", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly replicate use of the assessment in a token-economy application. Discussion confirms utility of the method but does not compare the specific earlier social-reinforcer or prompt-dependency effects. Methodological success alone is not a finding comparison.""}"
10.17605/osf.io/r8qp2,8109f7a62fdc044f,1,10.1177/0261927X12460666,The Language of Political Aggression,"Matsumoto, D., & Hwang, H. C.",2013,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": ""10.1177/0261927X12460666"", ""title"": ""The Language of Political Aggression"", ""authors"": ""Matsumoto, D., & Hwang, H. C."", ""year"": ""2013"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""mixed""}, ""quote"": ""We sought to conceptually replicate two studies of the role of linguistic style in\n\npredicting war attitudes and behaviors, Abe (2012) and Matsumoto and Hwang (2013), in\n\nthe U.S. Congressional context."", ""location"": ""Introduction, p. 6"", ""reason"": ""The paper explicitly replicates both Abe (2012) and Matsumoto and Hwang (2013), testing Matsumoto and Hwang (2013) through Hypotheses 1 and 3 across the congressional speech samples, so Matsumoto and Hwang (2013) is an omitted target that requires its own replication record with a mixed outcome.""}","{""authors"": ""Matsumoto, D., & Hwang, H. C."", ""year"": ""2013"", ""title"": ""The Language of Political Aggression"", ""doi"": ""10.1177/0261927X12460666"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1A, 1B, 2A, 2B, 3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""We sought to conceptually replicate two studies of the role of linguistic style in\n\npredicting war attitudes and behaviors, Abe (2012) and Matsumoto and Hwang (2013), in\n\nthe U.S. Congressional context."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We add to this area by replicating two\n\nprevious studies: Abe (2012) and Matsumoto and Hwang (2013) in the context of a\n\nlegislative body. The present study hypothesized that legislators who support war measures\n\nwould be externally focused and less cognitively complex in their speeches, while opponents\n\nof war measures would be internally focused. Speeches were obtained pertaining to the\n\ndecisions for the U.S. to take military action in Kosovo, Iraq, and Libya. While we found\n\nmixed results depending on the circumstances of a speciﬁc conﬂict, we demonstrate how\n\nautomated language analysis can be combined with voting records to better understand\n\nbehavioral action, such as legislative decision."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract explicitly frames the combined conceptual replications as mixed across conflicts; results and discussion report supported and unsupported hypotheses across samples.""}"
10.1002/hrdq.21464,4866281e6326e57b,0,,"Job attitudes and employee engagement: Considering the ""A-factor"".","Newman, D. A., Joseph, D. L., & Hulin, C. L.",2010,abstract,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 0, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": """", ""title"": ""Job attitudes and employee engagement: Considering the \""A-factor\""."", ""authors"": ""Newman, D. A., Joseph, D. L., & Hulin, C. L."", ""year"": ""2010"", ""type"": ""reproduction"", ""outcome_computation"": ""computationally reproducible"", ""outcome_robustness"": ""robustness challenges""}, ""quote"": ""Analyses not conducted in Newman et al. (2010) (Handbook of Employee Engagement: Perspectives, Issues, Research, and Practice, pp. 43–61) were performed to deconstruct the explained variance in work engagement into commonality coefficients representing all possible subsets of variables."", ""location"": ""abstract (L34); methods (L545-L547); results (L699-L731); discussion (L861-L875)"", ""reason"": ""Newman et al. (2010) is an omitted reproduction target: the authors explicitly re-estimated its structural equation model (Figure 4.3, Panel A) on its meta-analytic correlation matrix, recovering identical latent correlations, and then decomposed the model's variance to challenge its substantive conclusions regarding construct redundancy.""}","{""authors"": ""Newman, D. A., Joseph, D. L., & Hulin, C. L."", ""year"": ""2010"", ""title"": ""Job attitudes and employee engagement: Considering the \""A-factor\""."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""For the relationships among work engagement, job satisfaction, affective organizational commitment, and job involvement, we used the meta-analytic correlations from Newman et al. (2010). This allowed us to replicate and extend the work of Newman et al. by conducting new analyses on the same data."", ""outcome"": """", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": ""computationally reproducible"", ""outcome_computational_quote"": ""For example, our model produced estimated latent correlations from the A-factor of job attitudes to work engagement (φ=0.77), job satisfaction (φ=0.71), affective organizational commitment (φ=0.75), and job involvement (φ=0.72) that were identical to those reported by Newman et al. (2010). Only the RMSEA values differed slightly where our analysis yielded a value 0.006 lower than the value of 0.180 reported by Newman."", ""outcome_robustness"": ""robustness challenges"", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": ""Therefore, interpretation of relationships between factors such as those made by Newman et al. (2010) –and others –require analyses similar to those reported in the present study."", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Original meta-analytic data reanalysed with variance decomposition, challenging redundancy interpretation. Original 2010 numerical model explicitly recovered up to minor fit-index variation; numerical reproduction of 2011 is not separately established. Both publications are directly re-examined; 2010 supplied the data and original numerical model, while 2011 states the challenged claim. Partial full-text extraction limits certainty, especially the scope of the 2010 robustness verdict.""}"
10.1038/s41598-017-07404-4,4164f1a8792c6dbd,0,10.1016/S1474-4422(15)00338-5,Loci associated with ischaemic stroke and its subtypes (SiGN): a genome-wide association study,"NINDS Stroke Genetics Network (SiGN), I. S. G. C. (ISGC).",2015,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 0, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": ""10.1016/S1474-4422(15)00338-5"", ""title"": ""Loci associated with ischaemic stroke and its subtypes (SiGN): a genome-wide association study"", ""authors"": ""NINDS Stroke Genetics Network (SiGN), I. S. G. C. (ISGC)."", ""year"": ""2015"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""failed""}, ""quote"": ""We could not replicate the previously-established associations at the 1p13.2 (TSPAN2), 6p21.1 (SUPT3H/CDC5L), 7p21.1 (HDAC9), 9p21.3 (CDKN2B-AS1), 9q34 (ABO) and 12q24 (ALDH2) loci with overall IS or its subtypes."", ""location"": ""results"", ""reason"": ""In Table 1, Ref. 2 (NINDS SiGN 2015) is the explicit comparator re-tested for only two specific associations: rs12122341 at 1p13.2 (TSPAN2) with LAA (previously reported OR 1.19 [1.12–1.26], p = 1.30 × 10^-9; current study OR 1.09 [0.90–1.32], p = 0.99) and rs10744777 at 12q24 (ALDH2) with SVD (previously reported OR 1.17 [1.11–1.23], p = 2.92 × 10^-9; current study OR 1.08 [0.88–1.30], p = 1.00), both of which failed to replicate. The confirmed cardioembolic (CE) stroke associations in Table 1 explicitly re-test Ref. 9 (Kilarski et al. 2014) for 4q25 (PITX2) rs2634074 and Ref. 10 (Gudbjartsson et al. 2009) for 16q22 (ZFHX3) rs7193343, whereas the collective citations to Refs. 2–4 in the Discussion are background evidence-chain references without specific re-tested estimates from Ref. 2 for those confirmed CE variants; thus only the Table 1-assigned findings are covered for Ref. 2, resulting in a failed outcome.""}","{""authors"": ""NINDS Stroke Genetics Network (SiGN), I. S. G. C. (ISGC)."", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": ""Loci associated with ischaemic stroke and its subtypes (SiGN): a genome-wide association study"", ""doi"": ""10.1016/S1474-4422(15)00338-5"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We aimed to replicate the associations of eight loci with IS and/or its subtypes as previously reported in an independent set of patients with IS drawn from a Dutch cohort."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In a well-defined cohort of patients with IS, we confirmed the 4q25 (PITX2) and 16q22 (ZFHX3) loci to be significantly associated with the IS subtype CE. We also found locus 16q22 to be significantly associated with overall IS. We were not able to replicate the previously-established associations at the 1p13.2 (TSPAN2), 6p21.1 (SUPT3H/CDC5L), 7p21.1 (HDAC9), 9p21.3 (CDKN2B-AS1), 9q34 (ABO) and 12q24 (ALDH2) with overall IS or its subtypes although, barring locus 6p21.1 (SUPT3H/CDC5L), the effect direction of their associations were consistent with expectation2–11."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""TSPAN2 and ALDH2 associations failed; CE associations supported in discussion references 2–4, with additional subtype-specificity qualification. Identification follows Table 1 numbered reference mapping, corroborated by the discussion.""}"
10.1038/s41598-017-07404-4,4164f1a8792c6dbd,1,10.1002/ana.21480,Risk variants for atrial fibrillation on chromosome 4q25 associate with ischemic stroke,"Gretarsdottir, S. et al.",2008,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": ""10.1002/ana.21480"", ""title"": ""Risk variants for atrial fibrillation on chromosome 4q25 associate with ischemic stroke"", ""authors"": ""Gretarsdottir, S. et al."", ""year"": ""2008"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""failed""}, ""quote"": ""At the 4q25 (PITX2) locus, previously identified by GWAS, we confirmed the association at rs2634074 with CE stroke (OR = 1.43 for the T allele; 95% CI, 1.13–1.81, p = 0.029), but not at rs2200733 (OR = 1.30 for the T allele; 95% CI, 0.96–1.76; p = 0.60) (Table 1)."", ""location"": ""results"", ""reason"": ""Ref. 3 (Gretarsdottir et al. 2008) is an omitted target explicitly re-tested in Table 1 for the rs2200733 (4q25/PITX2) associations with overall IS and CE stroke, neither of which replicated.""}","{""authors"": ""Gretarsdottir, S. et al."", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": ""Risk variants for atrial fibrillation on chromosome 4q25 associate with ischemic stroke"", ""doi"": ""10.1002/ana.21480"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We aimed to replicate the associations of eight loci with IS and/or its subtypes as previously reported in an independent set of patients with IS drawn from a Dutch cohort."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""At the 4q25 (PITX2) locus, previously identified by GWAS, we confirmed the association at rs2634074 with CE stroke (OR = 1.43 for the T allele; 95% CI, 1.13–1.81, p = 0.029), but not at rs2200733 (OR = 1.30 for the T allele; 95% CI, 0.96–1.76; p = 0.60) (Table 1). "", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Table 1 assigns both rs2200733 associations to reference 3; neither replicated. Successful rs2634074 is assigned to reference 9. Identification follows Table 1 numbered reference mapping, corroborated by the discussion.""}"
10.1038/s41598-017-07404-4,4164f1a8792c6dbd,2,10.1016/S1474-4422(12)70234-X,Genetic risk factors for ischaemic stroke and its subtypes (the METASTROKE Collaboration): A meta-analysis of genome-wide association studies,"Traylor, M. et al.",2012,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 2, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": ""10.1016/S1474-4422(12)70234-X"", ""title"": ""Genetic risk factors for ischaemic stroke and its subtypes (the METASTROKE Collaboration): A meta-analysis of genome-wide association studies"", ""authors"": ""Traylor, M. et al."", ""year"": ""2012"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""failed""}, ""quote"": ""We could not replicate the previously-established associations at the 1p13.2 (TSPAN2), 6p21.1 (SUPT3H/CDC5L), 7p21.1 (HDAC9), 9p21.3 (CDKN2B-AS1), 9q34 (ABO) and 12q24 (ALDH2) loci with overall IS or its subtypes."", ""location"": ""results"", ""reason"": ""In Table 1, Ref. 4 (Traylor et al. 2012, METASTROKE) is the explicit comparator re-tested for only one specific association: rs11984041 at 7p21.1 (HDAC9) with LAA (previously reported OR 1.42 [1.28–1.57], p = 1.87 × 10^-11; current study OR 1.33 [1.04–1.72], p = 0.22), which failed to replicate. The confirmed cardioembolic (CE) stroke associations in Table 1 explicitly re-test Ref. 9 (Kilarski et al. 2014) for 4q25 (PITX2) rs2634074 and Ref. 10 (Gudbjartsson et al. 2009) for 16q22 (ZFHX3) rs7193343, while the collective citations to Refs. 2–4 in the Discussion serve as background evidence-chain context rather than re-tested comparators for those confirmed CE variants; therefore, only the Table 1-assigned HDAC9 LAA finding is covered for Ref. 4, yielding a failed outcome.""}","{""authors"": ""Traylor, M. et al."", ""year"": ""2012"", ""title"": ""Genetic risk factors for ischaemic stroke and its subtypes (the METASTROKE Collaboration): A meta-analysis of genome-wide association studies"", ""doi"": ""10.1016/S1474-4422(12)70234-X"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We aimed to replicate the associations of eight loci with IS and/or its subtypes as previously reported in an independent set of patients with IS drawn from a Dutch cohort."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In a well-defined cohort of patients with IS, we confirmed the 4q25 (PITX2) and 16q22 (ZFHX3) loci to be significantly associated with the IS subtype CE. We also found locus 16q22 to be significantly associated with overall IS. We were not able to replicate the previously-established associations at the 1p13.2 (TSPAN2), 6p21.1 (SUPT3H/CDC5L), 7p21.1 (HDAC9), 9p21.3 (CDKN2B-AS1), 9q34 (ABO) and 12q24 (ALDH2) with overall IS or its subtypes although, barring locus 6p21.1 (SUPT3H/CDC5L), the effect direction of their associations were consistent with expectation2–11."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""HDAC9 association failed; CE associations in discussion references 2–4 were confirmed. Identification follows Table 1 numbered reference mapping, corroborated by the discussion.""}"
10.1038/s41598-017-07404-4,4164f1a8792c6dbd,4,10.1002/ana.21590,Sequence variants on chromosome 9p21.3 confer risk for atherosclerotic stroke,"Gschwendtner, A. et al.",2009,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 4, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": ""10.1002/ana.21590"", ""title"": ""Sequence variants on chromosome 9p21.3 confer risk for atherosclerotic stroke"", ""authors"": ""Gschwendtner, A. et al."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""failed""}, ""quote"": ""We could not replicate the previously-established associations at the 1p13.2 (TSPAN2), 6p21.1 (SUPT3H/CDC5L), 7p21.1 (HDAC9), 9p21.3 (CDKN2B-AS1), 9q34 (ABO) and 12q24 (ALDH2) loci with overall IS or its subtypes."", ""location"": ""results"", ""reason"": ""Ref. 7 (Gschwendtner et al. 2009) is an omitted target explicitly re-tested in Table 1 for the 9p21.3 (CDKN2B-AS1) rs2383207 association with LAA stroke, which failed to replicate.""}","{""authors"": ""Gschwendtner, A. et al."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""Sequence variants on chromosome 9p21.3 confer risk for atherosclerotic stroke"", ""doi"": ""10.1002/ana.21590"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We aimed to replicate the associations of eight loci with IS and/or its subtypes as previously reported in an independent set of patients with IS drawn from a Dutch cohort."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We could not replicate the previously-established associations at the 1p13.2 (TSPAN2), 6p21.1 (SUPT3H/CDC5L), 7p21.1 (HDAC9), 9p21.3 (CDKN2B-AS1), 9q34 (ABO) and 12q24 (ALDH2) loci with overall IS or its subtypes."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""CDKN2B-AS1 association failed. Identification follows Table 1 numbered reference mapping, corroborated by the discussion.""}"
10.1038/s41598-017-07404-4,4164f1a8792c6dbd,5,10.1002/ana.23838,Ischemic stroke is associated with the ABO locus: the EuroCLOT study,"Williams, FMK. et al.",2013,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 5, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": ""10.1002/ana.23838"", ""title"": ""Ischemic stroke is associated with the ABO locus: the EuroCLOT study"", ""authors"": ""Williams, FMK. et al."", ""year"": ""2013"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""failed""}, ""quote"": ""We could not replicate the previously-established associations at the 1p13.2 (TSPAN2), 6p21.1 (SUPT3H/CDC5L), 7p21.1 (HDAC9), 9p21.3 (CDKN2B-AS1), 9q34 (ABO) and 12q24 (ALDH2) loci with overall IS or its subtypes."", ""location"": ""results"", ""reason"": ""Ref. 8 (Williams et al. 2013) is an omitted target explicitly re-tested in Table 1 for the 9q34 (ABO) rs505922 associations with overall IS, LAA, and CE, none of which replicated.""}","{""authors"": ""Williams, FMK. et al."", ""year"": ""2013"", ""title"": ""Ischemic stroke is associated with the ABO locus: the EuroCLOT study"", ""doi"": ""10.1002/ana.23838"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We aimed to replicate the associations of eight loci with IS and/or its subtypes as previously reported in an independent set of patients with IS drawn from a Dutch cohort."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We could not replicate the previously-established associations at the 1p13.2 (TSPAN2), 6p21.1 (SUPT3H/CDC5L), 7p21.1 (HDAC9), 9p21.3 (CDKN2B-AS1), 9q34 (ABO) and 12q24 (ALDH2) loci with overall IS or its subtypes."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""ABO associations failed. Identification follows Table 1 numbered reference mapping, corroborated by the discussion.""}"
10.1038/s41598-017-07404-4,4164f1a8792c6dbd,6,10.1212/WNL.0000000000000707,"Meta-analysis in more than 17,900 cases of ischemic stroke reveals a novel association at 12q24.12","Kilarski, LL. et al.",2014,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 6, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": ""10.1212/WNL.0000000000000707"", ""title"": ""Meta-analysis in more than 17,900 cases of ischemic stroke reveals a novel association at 12q24.12"", ""authors"": ""Kilarski, LL. et al."", ""year"": ""2014"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""mixed""}, ""quote"": ""In a well-defined cohort of patients with IS, we confirmed the 4q25 (PITX2) and 16q22 (ZFHX3) loci to be significantly associated with the IS subtype CE. We also found locus 16q22 to be significantly associated with overall IS. We were not able to replicate the previously-established associations at the 1p13.2 (TSPAN2), 6p21.1 (SUPT3H/CDC5L), 7p21.1 (HDAC9), 9p21.3 (CDKN2B-AS1), 9q34 (ABO) and 12q24 (ALDH2) with overall IS or its subtypes although, barring locus 6p21.1 (SUPT3H/CDC5L), the effect direction of their associations were consistent with expectation2–11."", ""location"": ""discussion"", ""reason"": ""Ref. 9 (Kilarski et al. 2014) is an omitted target explicitly re-tested in Table 1 for two associations: 12q24 (ALDH2) rs10744777 with overall IS (failed) and 4q25 (PITX2) rs2634074 with CE (confirmed), yielding a mixed outcome.""}","{""authors"": ""Kilarski, LL. et al."", ""year"": ""2014"", ""title"": ""Meta-analysis in more than 17,900 cases of ischemic stroke reveals a novel association at 12q24.12"", ""doi"": ""10.1212/WNL.0000000000000707"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We aimed to replicate the associations of eight loci with IS and/or its subtypes as previously reported in an independent set of patients with IS drawn from a Dutch cohort."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In a well-defined cohort of patients with IS, we confirmed the 4q25 (PITX2) and 16q22 (ZFHX3) loci to be significantly associated with the IS subtype CE. We also found locus 16q22 to be significantly associated with overall IS. We were not able to replicate the previously-established associations at the 1p13.2 (TSPAN2), 6p21.1 (SUPT3H/CDC5L), 7p21.1 (HDAC9), 9p21.3 (CDKN2B-AS1), 9q34 (ABO) and 12q24 (ALDH2) with overall IS or its subtypes although, barring locus 6p21.1 (SUPT3H/CDC5L), the effect direction of their associations were consistent with expectation2–11."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""ALDH2 overall IS association failed, while PITX2 rs2634074 CE association succeeded; both map to reference 9 in Table 1. Identification follows Table 1 numbered reference mapping, corroborated by the discussion.""}"
10.1038/s41598-017-07404-4,4164f1a8792c6dbd,7,10.1038/ng.417,A sequence variant in ZFHX3 on 16q22 associates with atrial fibrillation and ischemic stroke,"Gudbjartsson, DF. et al.",2009,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 7, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": ""10.1038/ng.417"", ""title"": ""A sequence variant in ZFHX3 on 16q22 associates with atrial fibrillation and ischemic stroke"", ""authors"": ""Gudbjartsson, DF. et al."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""successful""}, ""quote"": ""We also replicated 16q22 (ZFHX3) as a risk locus for CE (OR = 1.62 for the T allele; 95% CI, 1.26–2.07, p = 0.0014) and found this locus significantly associated with overall IS (OR = 1.28 for the T allele; 95% CI, 1.12–1.47, p = 0.002) (Table 1)."", ""location"": ""results"", ""reason"": ""Ref. 10 (Gudbjartsson et al. 2009) is an omitted target explicitly re-tested in Table 1 for the 16q22 (ZFHX3) rs7193343 associations with overall IS and CE stroke, both of which were confirmed.""}","{""authors"": ""Gudbjartsson, DF. et al."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""A sequence variant in ZFHX3 on 16q22 associates with atrial fibrillation and ischemic stroke"", ""doi"": ""10.1038/ng.417"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We aimed to replicate the associations of eight loci with IS and/or its subtypes as previously reported in an independent set of patients with IS drawn from a Dutch cohort."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We also replicated 16q22 (ZFHX3) as a risk locus for CE (OR = 1.62 for the T allele; 95% CI, 1.26–2.07, p = 0.0014) and found this locus significantly associated with overall IS (OR = 1.28 for the T allele; 95% CI, 1.12–1.47, p = 0.002) (Table 1). "", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""ZFHX3 associations with overall IS and CE replicated, as originally reported in reference 10. Identification follows Table 1 numbered reference mapping, corroborated by the discussion.""}"
